WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.074
Do you want subtitles for any video?
-=[ ai.OpenSubtitles.com ]=-

2
00:01:03.700 --> 00:02:21.420
A trick to break the walls of Troy. it's in the ground.

3
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:29.180
Common gods respect their offering.

4
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.980
Save it from the waves. take it to Athena's temple.

5
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.060
The war is over.

6
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.380
Not this time.

7
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:17.300
Tell them if who's wine they're drinking.

8
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.580
I don't want Odysseus to song. I want Odysseus.

9
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.380
Then why do you entertain suitors in his haremless night after night?

10
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.700
In this world. a man does what he chooses.

11
00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:29.780
I do what I can.

12
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:30.980
I am a man.

13
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:31.540
No. Mom.

14
00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:33.780
So throw them out.

15
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:38.020
Break Zeus' law and see what they do to you if you give them that excuse.

16
00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.870
I've already lost a husband.

17
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:47.790
I don't want to lose a son.

18
00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:49.950
Dad's coming back.

19
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:50.670
Where is he?

20
00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.430
Eight years since Troy fell.

21
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.350
Almost 20 since he left us.

22
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:56.910
Where is he?

23
00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:11.490
All of you.

24
00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.770
Are you calling us beggars?

25
00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.090
I'm saying you've been welcome here for three years.

26
00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.130
We've only enjoyed your hospitality for so long.

27
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.360
Because your mother won't choose.

28
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.760
She's already married.

29
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:24.640
Odysseus is dead.

30
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:26.880
What do you know about my dad?

31
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.350
I know he kept stringing cattle with bad wine.

32
00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.550
You had the best of it the first two seasons.

33
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:36.990
And I know he never came home.

34
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.190
Not yet.

35
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.830
You're pining for a daddy you didn't even know.

36
00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.710
Like some sniveling bastard.

37
00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.910
Well since none of us can really know who our fathers are.

38
00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.030
don't claim that I'm lying.

39
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:51.630
But I did know him.

40
00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.390
As boys.

41
00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:54.270
We watched him hunt.

42
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.120
I tried to volunteer for his expedition to Troy.

43
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.600
He told me to stay here and keep an eye on you.

44
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.200
Me or my mother?

45
00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:06.670
You can't blame me for looking.

46
00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:08.430
My dad is coming home.

47
00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.230
And his hospitality won't be as generous as hers.

48
00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.830
So he doesn't follow Zeus' law then?

49
00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:16.350
Zeus' law tells us to treat strangers as we'd be treated

50
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.190
because they might be gods in disguise.

51
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.550
I think we've established. Antinous. that you are not a god.

52
00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.830
This one certainly doesn't smell like a god.

53
00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.070
Wouldn't be much of a disguise if he did.

54
00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:27.550
Careful.

55
00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.510
He might strike you down right now.

56
00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.160
That thunder was luck.

57
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:47.920
I don't know.

58
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.920
You have wise eyes.

59
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.320
Seen his eyes.

60
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:52.880
Here.

61
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:53.840
You sailed with traitors?

62
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.240
From Sparta.

63
00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:57.760
Has Menelaus come home from the war?

64
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.320
Years ago.

65
00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.600
Odysseus never came back?

66
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.600
We don't even know if he's alive.

67
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:04.720
Have you heard anything?

68
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.560
The song of how he won the war.

69
00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:07.440
We've all heard that.

70
00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.000
Anything else?

71
00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:08.960
No.

72
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.960
But Sparta is full of Trojan war veterans.

73
00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.000
Someone will know something.

74
00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.160
You're Odysseus' son?

75
00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.080
Go to Sparta.

76
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:21.280
Go.

77
00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:22.880
See Menelaus.

78
00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:24.720
He'd welcome you.

79
00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.590
The queen wants to speak to the stranger.

80
00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.550
She lifts her nose.

81
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:36.820
Just don't try to give her any false hope.

82
00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.660
She has some way of knowing whether a person

83
00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.420
has actually seen Odysseus.

84
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:41.700
You see that man?

85
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.460
He can't see much anymore but that doesn't

86
00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.620
stop him beating travelers to death

87
00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.620
in exchange for lies about my father.

88
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:16.640
Too fast.

89
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.720
How can you know?

90
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.520
You don't need eyes to hear a hasty combination.

91
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.520
Mentor push him.

92
00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:32.940
Too fast.

93
00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:43.620
He's as friendly as you.

94
00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.420
He only answered to your father.

95
00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.180
Where'd he get him from?

96
00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.100
He met right here.

97
00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:00.460
I'll take him.

98
00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:01.740
For your boy?

99
00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:03.660
For a hunting dog.

100
00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.260
Damned if he wasn't right.

101
00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:44.640
But still. how bad a scar do you want?

102
00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.000
Why do you always pluck your bow?

103
00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:49.840
It's only fair.

104
00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.800
To warn your prey?

105
00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.560
To fight with honor.

106
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.000
But you weren't fighting. you were hunting.

107
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.120
Hunting's nothing but fighting with stealth.

108
00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:57.600
Clever.

109
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.480
Clever but your cleverness is gonna get you in trouble.

110
00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:02.000
Try again.

111
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.120
This time not as fast or precise.

112
00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.520
You can't win with a perfect defense.

113
00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.040
Draw the attack.

114
00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:09.540
How?

115
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:10.800
Hesitate.

116
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.680
Open your belly to your opponent's blade.

117
00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.600
Their attack becomes your chance.

118
00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.780
You teach my father that?

119
00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:49.500
No. no.

120
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.300
He taught me.

121
00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:55.660
He said you're not always going to be the strongest or the fastest.

122
00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.420
So be the smartest.

123
00:09:58.380 --> 00:09:59.260
What would he do. huh?

124
00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.780
If he came back here to find all these suitors in his house?

125
00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:03.020
Don't.

126
00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:06.220
I can't even let myself imagine what he'd do to them.

127
00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.020
There are so many.

128
00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.180
Cut a couple down. the others cave. bullies and cowards.

129
00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:13.820
Most of them were old enough to go to Troy.

130
00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:20.350
but when they saw Agamemnon sail. their fathers hit them.

131
00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:27.860
And you knew Agamemnon's war would take many years and many lives.

132
00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.260
So he raised his army by lottery.

133
00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:38.880
Name?

134
00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:39.680
Sinon.

135
00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:40.400
Draw.

136
00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:40.640
No.

137
00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:42.000
Name?

138
00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:42.720
Antinous.

139
00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:43.220
Draw.

140
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:46.400
Yes.

141
00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:46.960
By lots?

142
00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.400
Antinous told me he volunteered.

143
00:10:52.400 --> 00:11:04.480
Your boy's trying to take Sinon's place.

144
00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.480
I'm not going to let him.

145
00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.640
He needs to stay here. take care of his family.

146
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.360
You can watch over your boy. too.

147
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:13.760
Of course.

148
00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:19.020
He's with me.

149
00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.220
He wanted Antinous to look after me.

150
00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:22.860
No. no. no.

151
00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.540
But Odysseus was sensitive to a father's shame.

152
00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:27.660
He fell for his father's.

153
00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:31.660
You and your mother meant more to him than promises of glory.

154
00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:34.380
So he knew it would be years.

155
00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:36.780
Did he tell my mother?

156
00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:38.940
That's a question for her.

157
00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.740
You may ask. is he ever coming back?

158
00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.180
Hargis thinks so.

159
00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:46.860
Never known a dog reads 20.

160
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.980
But he's long outlived his master.

161
00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.750
I didn't mean to stop you.

162
00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:17.620
The elders decreed when I finish your grandfather's burial shroud.

163
00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:18.900
it'll be time to remarry.

164
00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:19.780
Why?

165
00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.810
Ithaca's been without a king too long.

166
00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:29.490
They want security.

167
00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:31.250
From what?

168
00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:32.130
People from the sea.

169
00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:34.530
That's just Traveler's Tales.

170
00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.330
All we know of the world is Traveler's Tales.

171
00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:38.930
That's how we found out Troy had fallen.

172
00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:42.770
Now they all tell stories of attacks by people from the sea.

173
00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:45.810
And who are the people from the sea?

174
00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:47.330
No one knows.

175
00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.410
But the elders wanted the good to be prepared.

176
00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:50.050
Prepared?

177
00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:53.330
Part of the greatest civilization ever known.

178
00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:56.060
Look at this great house.

179
00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.220
It's known as old as knowledge.

180
00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.820
Had the mercy of some greedy. unwanted guests.

181
00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:07.740
The structure's nothing without people's respect for its meaning.

182
00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.620
Zeus's law.

183
00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:11.340
Treat others as you would be treated.

184
00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.020
And they abuse that.

185
00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.220
They destroy everything.

186
00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:17.980
So how could you think of marrying one of them?

187
00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:22.700
Because a long time ago. your father asked me to.

188
00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.100
Hagamemnon is our king.

189
00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.660
The Trojans took his brother to life.

190
00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.180
Or she ran away with them.

191
00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:31.740
Either way. the walls of Troy

192
00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:33.180
are said to be impregnable.

193
00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.100
We'll sit outside that great city.

194
00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:38.860
trying to draw them into battle.

195
00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:44.080
As the years go by. the Telemachus grows.

196
00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.240
And you learn life without me.

197
00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.960
Menelaus will give up on Helen.

198
00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.980
This is Hagamemnon's excuse

199
00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.100
to break Troy's control of the trading routes.

200
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.290
Won't let it pass.

201
00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:23.180
If he refused. he would never.

202
00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:27.820
He sacrificed his own daughter for favorable wins.

203
00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.940
God's want his daughter's life.

204
00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:33.660
The power of a sacrifice

205
00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:38.060
is in the cost to the person making it.

206
00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:51.360
Return soon.

207
00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:53.520
If at all.

208
00:14:54.080 --> 00:15:06.940
The brightest crew and headed for the horizon.

209
00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.940
held hands on deck as we rode west at dusk.

210
00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.580
chasing the escaping sun.

211
00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:22.900
There's a world beyond these walls.

212
00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:23.860
Vengeance.

213
00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:46.140
You'll take another husband.

214
00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:50.190
Enough sacrifice.

215
00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:51.950
Love's not a sacrifice.

216
00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:52.830
Of course it is.

217
00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:55.660
I want you to make it.

218
00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:56.620
It's not up to you.

219
00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:07.250
No.

220
00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:08.930
Promise me you'll come back.

221
00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:24.180
Then your memory won't be making my decisions.

222
00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:36.400
I'm not sure it suits me.

223
00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.720
If the people from the sea are real.

224
00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:55.600
right now. Ithaca has no king or he's an army.

225
00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:57.680
I could take this role.

226
00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:25.470
That's not fair.

227
00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.430
You said only he could string this bow.

228
00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:30.350
He was happy to let others try.

229
00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:34.370
He'd line up all the axes.

230
00:17:35.010 --> 00:18:11.300
Mom. they're bleeding us dry.

231
00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:14.580
so you'll be forced to choose one of them before I can claim the throne myself.

232
00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.580
The traveler told me Menelaus returned years ago.

233
00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:19.540
They've all returned.

234
00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:20.740
All who survived.

235
00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:23.620
Maybe he is dead.

236
00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:26.420
And if he is. then this is my palace.

237
00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:29.060
We need to know.

238
00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.620
I have to go to Menelaus to find out the truth.

239
00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.300
Do that because these suitors mean you harm.

240
00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:36.660
Oh. you think I don't know that?

241
00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:40.580
Dad. every night they push me to give them the excuse they need.

242
00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:43.140
I'm out there on the sea. on the road.

243
00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:50.590
You don't want me to claim the throne myself.

244
00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:56.750
You just want to keep me in my place so that you can stay queen of Ithaca.

245
00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.870
I've had enough of being queen of Ithaca without my king.

246
00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:03.950
Without your king or a king?

247
00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:10.740
You're ready to take your father's place. your child.

248
00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:23.730
Too loud. Melanthius.

249
00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.370
Don't wear your spurs in the palace. you're a cowherd.

250
00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:27.570
Not a horse.

251
00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:28.930
And you're a pig farmer.

252
00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.690
And who are you to talk to me like that?

253
00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:34.690
A servant our master left in charge of his herdsmen.

254
00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.370
The master is long gone. you blind old fool.

255
00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.970
And you're about as welcome here as that dog is.

256
00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:43.430
Argus!

257
00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:47.920
Argus!

258
00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:50.480
Argus!

259
00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:51.680
Argus!

260
00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:55.810
Say. he didn't do anything?

261
00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:56.850
So that stink is you?

262
00:19:58.290 --> 00:19:59.410
Leave the old dog alone.

263
00:20:00.050 --> 00:20:00.770
Which one?

264
00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:01.410
Both of us.

265
00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:03.970
What did you say?

266
00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:05.090
He just got carried away.

267
00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:07.010
Argus is my father's hunting dog.

268
00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:07.810
His hunting dog?

269
00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:13.060
Look at that thing.

270
00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:16.180
Get it out of here. we're trying to-

271
00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.620
Don't! Can't you see he's in pain?

272
00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:18.840
Okay.

273
00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:21.300
You might put it out of his misery.

274
00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:22.180
Leave him alone!

275
00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:53.330
My son!

276
00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:57.710
Easy. easy.

277
00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.430
Young pup lost his head.

278
00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:01.150
Show some mercy.

279
00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:05.180
Drink some more of his wine.

280
00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:05.980
I'll take care of this.

281
00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:08.780
It's my father's hunting dog.

282
00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.380
He's only living to see Odysseus return.

283
00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:13.260
He's fine.

284
00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.780
I'll put him on the dung heap out front so he can greet his master a little sooner.

285
00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:28.770
That dog stays out there!

286
00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:33.220
Next time. think twice before you rough up your life for an animal.

287
00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:51.230
You're clear.

288
00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:52.750
He's the only people you trust.

289
00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:54.510
You pricked your mother's heart.

290
00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:56.590
If I stay. they'll force me to fight.

291
00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:57.950
No one will back me.

292
00:21:57.950 --> 00:21:59.950
I have to find out what happened to my father.

293
00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:03.550
Ithaca will be behind me if they know Odysseus is dead.

294
00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.150
His suitors will never know that.

295
00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:06.510
You will never make it back alive again.

296
00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.710
Tell Lintor to meet me at the ships just before dawn.

297
00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:12.990
What do you want me to tell your mother?

298
00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:15.390
To hold out for as long as she can.

299
00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:21.090
Ships ready. tides right.

300
00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.490
Have I ever told you that you have Y's eyes. Athena's eyes?

301
00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:27.410
Once or twice.

302
00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:29.490
You haven't even asked me where we're going yet.

303
00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:30.370
Where are we going?

304
00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:31.970
I'll find my dad.

305
00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:33.490
He's alive.

306
00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:35.250
I can feel it in the wind and the waves.

307
00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:36.610
He's out there somewhere.

308
00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:38.370
Wandering. injured. lost.

309
00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:38.850
I don't know.

310
00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:40.850
But he's alive.

311
00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:13.630
More dreams?

312
00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:16.350
Men drowning.

313
00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:22.670
Inside some dark space.

314
00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:27.440
Firelight on stone.

315
00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:27.920
I don't know.

316
00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:32.660
Don't you want to remember?

317
00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:35.060
Why can't God speak in ways we understand?

318
00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:39.550
Who doesn't understand thunder?

319
00:23:40.510 --> 00:23:41.310
The fire.

320
00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:43.710
A child's smile.

321
00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:44.430
Good harvest.

322
00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:46.750
You hide yourselves from us.

323
00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:49.440
Look.

324
00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:51.820
Who's moving that cloud?

325
00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:54.300
Massing those birds?

326
00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:57.100
Who's moving the water?

327
00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:00.590
Who were you talking to?

328
00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.140
Athena came to me again.

329
00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.300
She's telling me to remember.

330
00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:10.050
To leave.

331
00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:12.210
Why would you leave?

332
00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:13.490
We have everything we need.

333
00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:15.410
How long have I been here. Calypso?

334
00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:21.630
I have a home somewhere.

335
00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:26.050
There's someone out there.

336
00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:28.050
How do you know?

337
00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:29.810
I don't know.

338
00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:30.690
I feel.

339
00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:51.600
What we have here could last forever.

340
00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:52.800
Forever is for God's.

341
00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:54.400
Are you a God?

342
00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:58.430
You want to remember.

343
00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.830
but what if remembering destroys your happiness?

344
00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:02.670
Then it wasn't real.

345
00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:03.570
No.

346
00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:12.510
No more.

347
00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:13.070
But I love it.

348
00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:15.310
Stop eating the lotus.

349
00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:17.230
Tell me what you want.

350
00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:18.670
Tell me what you remember.

351
00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.260
After Troy.

352
00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:32.060
We won the war.

353
00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:33.740
And then what?

354
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:41.760
Ten years on this fucking beach.

355
00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:47.860
Let's go home.

356
00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:51.140
Remember who we leave here.

357
00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:57.460
Do we have enough provisions?

358
00:26:58.500 --> 00:26:59.860
We've taken enough from Troy.

359
00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.500
Another stop or two won't kill us.

360
00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:15.760
It's still the oars.

361
00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:27.410
We'll raise the sail.

362
00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:28.690
Follow the southerly winds.

363
00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:29.190
Yes. sir.

364
00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.170
Agamemnon's rowing west on the known provisions route.

365
00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:34.530
He's got more mouths to feed.

366
00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.170
We'll find places to land.

367
00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:38.530
Are you sure it's a risk?

368
00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:40.130
I've followed Agamemnon long enough.

369
00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.850
With a little luck and the right winds.

370
00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:44.530
we'll see some of the world

371
00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.410
still be home before Agamemnon.

372
00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:58.290
Just keep the bear on the right.

373
00:29:15.460 --> 00:29:16.500
Lucky bastard.

374
00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:19.380
We're down to our last supplies.

375
00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.900
Assemble a landing party.

376
00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:21.700
Food and water.

377
00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:22.660
Enough for three weeks.

378
00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:24.420
What if it won't give it?

379
00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:28.020
How badly do you want to get home?

380
00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:30.740
Get your weapons ready.

381
00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:31.540
Raging.

382
00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:32.180
Move it.

383
00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:32.900
Harrowing.

384
00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:34.900
Pondering.

385
00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:40.880
Ask him why they abandoned their village

386
00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:42.080
instead of welcoming us.

387
00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:43.600
Why didn't you welcome us?

388
00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:45.360
We didn't know.

389
00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:47.920
We were passing by for the sea people.

390
00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:49.040
You're not one of them.

391
00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.640
This says they thought we were the sea people.

392
00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:51.140
No.

393
00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:52.720
We're Greeks.

394
00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:54.400
From Ithaca.

395
00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.720
Maybe you heard of our victory at Troy.

396
00:29:57.920 --> 00:29:59.760
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

397
00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:02.400
If you're going to show your loyalty.

398
00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:03.840
I hope you didn't do it.

399
00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:06.960
You won't like what you'll find there.

400
00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:08.160
He says if we go that way.

401
00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:10.160
we won't like the things we'll find.

402
00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:13.260
After years of war.

403
00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.540
no one could stand between my men

404
00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:17.980
and home.

405
00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:20.540
Not even me.

406
00:30:20.540 --> 00:30:23.570
Something happened?

407
00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:51.790
Key bastard.

408
00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:32.580
Someone lives here.

409
00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:35.220
Yeah. they make cheese.

410
00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:54.860
Pretty good.

411
00:32:58.620 --> 00:32:59.820
There's a lot of it.

412
00:32:59.900 --> 00:33:00.540
We should leave.

413
00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:02.940
We've brought nothing to trade with.

414
00:33:03.900 --> 00:33:05.100
Zeus' law.

415
00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:06.220
I don't know if whoever lives here

416
00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:07.580
has heard of Zeus' law.

417
00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:08.940
Or Zeus for that matter.

418
00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:10.460
Everyone's heard of Zeus' law.

419
00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:12.540
Get the fire going.

420
00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:13.740
We'll welcome the owner back.

421
00:34:33.490 --> 00:34:34.850
Get away from the fire.

422
00:34:34.850 --> 00:37:29.120
Let's out its flock.

423
00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:30.240
We'll rush the entrance.

424
00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:31.840
Rest up.

425
00:37:31.840 --> 00:38:21.390
Jam something in the cave mouth.

426
00:38:21.390 --> 00:38:22.110
Leave a gap.

427
00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:23.890
Maybe.

428
00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:25.810
But what?

429
00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:27.330
There's law.

430
00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:32.310
It'll notice.

431
00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:36.020
But grab the thin end.

432
00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:36.820
put it in the fire.

433
00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:39.140
How much rope do we have?

434
00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:41.220
How big's the flock?

435
00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:42.260
A couple dozen. maybe.

436
00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:46.000
That's too many of us.

437
00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:48.240
When it comes back.

438
00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:49.440
two of us have to get out.

439
00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:50.560
Or at least get killed.

440
00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:57.250
Run low through the flock

441
00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:58.290
towards the entrance

442
00:38:58.290 --> 00:38:59.410
and then split wide.

443
00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:01.250
So we can only grab one of them?

444
00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:01.750
Right.

445
00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:03.250
Four of us should make the run.

446
00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:04.690
Two and two.

447
00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:06.210
Why risk more men?

448
00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:07.650
He ate two of us last night.

449
00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:09.170
But one other two before he sleeps.

450
00:39:34.450 --> 00:39:34.950
Go. go. go!

451
00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:16.690
What about it?

452
00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:18.050
We just leave him here to run?

453
00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:21.040
Has food for that thing?

454
00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:24.850
Dead Odysseus.

455
00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:26.930
He died helping us escape.

456
00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:28.290
We'll honor him by escaping.

457
00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:43.620
He can talk.

458
00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:46.580
Why didn't he talk before?

459
00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:49.620
Do you talk to ants?

460
00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:53.140
Should we reason with it?

461
00:41:53.140 --> 00:41:56.880
We're past that.

462
00:41:56.880 --> 00:43:09.020
What are you doing?

463
00:43:10.620 --> 00:43:11.180
No!

464
00:43:11.180 --> 00:44:56.780
You lost good soldiers to that beast.

465
00:44:56.780 --> 00:44:57.740
Yeah. and that beast

466
00:44:58.300 --> 00:44:59.500
was the son of Poseidon.

467
00:45:00.140 --> 00:45:00.640
What?

468
00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:02.220
When we put its eye out.

469
00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:03.820
we heard it crying his name.

470
00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:05.500
That thing was a monster.

471
00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:06.460
I heard it too.

472
00:45:07.580 --> 00:45:09.420
So now the sea is against us?

473
00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:10.220
And the wind.

474
00:45:11.420 --> 00:45:12.540
Why are we supposed to get home

475
00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:13.980
with Poseidon against us?

476
00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:16.380
We can't live by omens and sacrifices.

477
00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:19.710
Do you know how many times

478
00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:20.990
I've heard you weeping.

479
00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:22.750
praying to Athena since we left Troy?

480
00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:24.590
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

481
00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:26.930
Take this.

482
00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:30.230
Wear it.

483
00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:32.770
To remember those we couldn't bury.

484
00:45:34.690 --> 00:45:36.610
The gods helped those who helped themselves.

485
00:45:40.560 --> 00:45:41.280
From that moment.

486
00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:44.240
every rough sea or headwind

487
00:45:44.240 --> 00:45:45.600
told the crew we were cursed.

488
00:45:48.350 --> 00:45:49.390
But where is my crew?

489
00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:53.200
When will you let me see my men?

490
00:45:54.480 --> 00:45:55.680
If I could just talk to them.

491
00:45:55.680 --> 00:45:56.640
they'd help me remember

492
00:45:57.200 --> 00:45:58.400
that I have a wife.

493
00:45:58.400 --> 00:45:59.600
children. maybe a son.

494
00:46:00.160 --> 00:46:01.200
How long have I been here?

495
00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:04.020
A long time.

496
00:46:05.140 --> 00:46:06.100
If I had a son.

497
00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:09.660
how old would he be now?

498
00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:13.630
I don't know how to speak to a king.

499
00:46:14.110 --> 00:46:14.750
You'll be fine.

500
00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:16.510
Keep your mouth shut.

501
00:46:17.710 --> 00:46:19.150
But if anyone asks.

502
00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:21.230
we're traitors from Crete.

503
00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:21.870
Then we'll figure out

504
00:46:21.870 --> 00:46:22.910
how to approach the king.

505
00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:33.750
Welcome.

506
00:46:34.530 --> 00:46:34.930
Thank you.

507
00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:36.610
You seem to be having quite-

508
00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:37.110
This way.

509
00:46:43.070 --> 00:46:44.270
Where are you taking us?

510
00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:45.470
The king insists on

511
00:46:45.470 --> 00:46:46.670
meeting strangers himself.

512
00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:13.940
Sid. not you.

513
00:47:13.940 --> 00:47:20.690
You're a boy of hospitality.

514
00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:21.730
As Zeus demands.

515
00:47:22.690 --> 00:47:24.290
the aircraft should tell us who you are.

516
00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:27.730
You'll take the right day to arrive.

517
00:47:27.730 --> 00:47:29.170
Feast on my daughter's wedding.

518
00:47:29.810 --> 00:47:30.850
Tomorrow she leaves

519
00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:31.970
to start a new life.

520
00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:34.610
You don't look like a beggar.

521
00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:36.930
Imagine you know who I am.

522
00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:39.440
I'm not last.

523
00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:41.200
The son of Atreus.

524
00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:43.120
Brother of Agamemnon.

525
00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:45.600
The husband of Helen.

526
00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:47.840
Perhaps you've heard of Helen.

527
00:47:50.700 --> 00:47:51.200
Face.

528
00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:53.660
I launched a thousand ships.

529
00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:57.940
That may be now just 500.

530
00:47:57.940 --> 00:48:04.850
Left on the battlefields of Troy

531
00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:05.970
for that face.

532
00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:07.810
For your brother's ambition.

533
00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:10.530
This one looks familiar.

534
00:48:11.970 --> 00:48:12.610
Hey. Jax.

535
00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:15.920
Achilles.

536
00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:18.860
I don't miss him as much as one

537
00:48:18.860 --> 00:48:19.900
particular warrior.

538
00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:22.460
He was the smartest of us all.

539
00:48:25.340 --> 00:48:27.180
Ten years we rotted on that beach.

540
00:48:41.020 --> 00:48:44.670
Deadlocked to one man.

541
00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:54.780
You ever hear the story of the horse?

542
00:48:54.780 --> 00:49:00.380
What did you hear from the inside. man?

543
00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:05.410
We were in there for days.

544
00:49:06.850 --> 00:49:09.330
We squeezed inside before the ship set sail.

545
00:49:10.290 --> 00:49:12.210
And as yet he knew that no one could know

546
00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:13.010
that we were inside.

547
00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:14.050
Not even sinning.

548
00:49:14.050 --> 00:49:15.730
The soldier we left behind.

549
00:49:17.970 --> 00:49:23.140
The first two tides are men drowned

550
00:49:23.140 --> 00:49:24.340
right in front of us.

551
00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:31.070
Are they silent?

552
00:49:31.870 --> 00:49:33.070
Or piss and shit?

553
00:49:36.670 --> 00:50:01.950
A gift.

554
00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:10.210
Some gods respect their offering.

555
00:50:10.210 --> 00:57:41.540
When did you last see a free...

556
00:57:41.540 --> 00:57:46.430
Righteous.

557
00:57:47.710 --> 00:57:49.710
The gods granted our righteous victory

558
00:57:50.030 --> 00:57:51.630
but did not bless our voyages.

559
00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:54.350
Took me years to get home.

560
00:57:55.310 --> 00:57:56.350
And he didn't.

561
00:57:56.350 --> 00:57:57.870
Some did and wish they hadn't.

562
00:57:59.070 --> 00:57:59.550
Tell him.

563
00:58:00.590 --> 00:58:01.870
Tell him about your brother.

564
00:58:02.590 --> 00:58:03.710
About the fate to the gods

565
00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:05.950
bestowed on him after your righteous struggle.

566
00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:07.870
About his hero's welcome.

567
00:58:07.870 --> 00:58:11.150
About how Agamemnon made it home years before us.

568
00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:13.230
Tell him how he was welcomed by his queen.

569
00:58:13.230 --> 00:58:15.150
my twin sister Clytemnestra.

570
00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:16.110
No. no.

571
00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:16.830
Tell him how she...

572
00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:17.550
No!

573
00:58:17.630 --> 00:58:19.470
He does not want to hear about my brother!

574
00:58:29.710 --> 00:58:30.990
Not from any of your men.

575
00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:34.830
Or Agamemnon's were traitors to travelers.

576
00:58:34.830 --> 00:58:35.870
You come here for somers.

577
00:58:35.870 --> 00:58:37.070
Is that what you're interested in?

578
00:58:37.070 --> 00:58:37.570
Gossip.

579
00:58:38.510 --> 00:58:40.030
You're in the right place.

580
00:58:40.030 --> 00:58:42.510
Anyone want to spin a story about Odysseus?

581
00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:45.630
Some say he's rich or some say poor.

582
00:58:47.310 --> 00:58:48.430
Some say he perished.

583
00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:49.870
Some say he's in prison.

584
00:58:49.870 --> 00:58:50.750
What say you?

585
00:58:50.750 --> 00:58:51.550
In prison.

586
00:58:51.550 --> 00:58:55.630
Out of prison.

587
00:58:59.810 --> 00:59:01.490
Tell me what you do remember.

588
00:59:01.490 --> 00:59:06.290
Men became more and more convinced

589
00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:07.970
that I defended Poseidon.

590
00:59:07.970 --> 01:00:56.300
We're here in peace.

591
01:00:59.500 --> 01:01:01.020
We just need some food.

592
01:01:01.020 --> 01:01:07.890
We need food.

593
01:01:09.810 --> 01:01:10.850
Can you understand that?

594
01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:11.650
It's just a child.

595
01:01:14.850 --> 01:01:16.610
If that's a child. what about its parents?

596
01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:20.920
It's okay.

597
01:01:21.940 --> 01:01:22.440
No. no. no. no.

598
01:01:23.060 --> 01:05:30.700
Follow the ghost.

599
01:05:31.900 --> 01:05:32.940
What if they have ships?

600
01:05:32.940 --> 01:05:35.020
We need to get as far away as possible.

601
01:05:42.660 --> 01:05:43.700
We've lost our way.

602
01:05:44.740 --> 01:05:50.530
We need to find food and get our bearings.

603
01:05:50.530 --> 01:05:51.570
Turn to port.

604
01:05:51.570 --> 01:06:23.620
A nitrogen at the beach!

605
01:06:36.590 --> 01:06:37.790
Form a landing party.

606
01:06:40.480 --> 01:06:40.980
Tomorrow.

607
01:06:43.950 --> 01:06:44.910
Tomorrow.

608
01:06:55.570 --> 01:06:56.930
Leave someone with the tenders.

609
01:06:58.370 --> 01:06:59.490
We think you should stay.

610
01:07:02.290 --> 01:07:03.090
We?

611
01:07:03.090 --> 01:07:03.590
All of us.

612
01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:05.330
Still alive.

613
01:07:16.660 --> 01:07:17.620
Is this a mutiny?

614
01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:18.980
That's up to you.

615
01:07:24.050 --> 01:07:24.930
Give me your boat.

616
01:07:24.930 --> 01:07:26.210
I'll make sure we eat tonight.

617
01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:28.130
No matter what you find.

618
01:09:15.500 --> 01:09:16.620
No. no. it's okay.

619
01:09:16.620 --> 01:09:17.120
It's okay.

620
01:09:23.570 --> 01:09:24.210
It's all right.

621
01:09:24.210 --> 01:09:25.410
No one's gonna hurt you.

622
01:09:25.410 --> 01:09:26.930
If I do what you want.

623
01:09:26.930 --> 01:09:28.850
No one is going to hurt you.

624
01:09:32.900 --> 01:09:33.940
My name's Urilicus.

625
01:09:33.940 --> 01:09:34.980
What's your name?

626
01:09:34.980 --> 01:09:35.480
Cersei.

627
01:09:36.100 --> 01:09:36.600
Cersei?

628
01:09:37.220 --> 01:09:38.180
We need food.

629
01:09:38.180 --> 01:09:38.680
Badly.

630
01:09:39.940 --> 01:09:40.740
We have food.

631
01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:06.350
You let me.

632
01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:10.880
Your weapons.

633
01:10:10.880 --> 01:10:11.520
Your armor.

634
01:10:13.360 --> 01:10:14.400
Put it out the back.

635
01:10:55.420 --> 01:10:58.700
You like my meat?

636
01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:00.320
Why?

637
01:11:00.940 --> 01:11:01.900
Do you have so much?

638
01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:05.180
You never know when strangers will come.

639
01:11:16.030 --> 01:11:16.530
Or them.

640
01:11:19.500 --> 01:11:20.780
What's their appetites?

641
01:11:22.860 --> 01:11:24.300
Too much is never enough.

642
01:12:40.880 --> 01:12:42.400
URILICUS!

643
01:12:42.400 --> 01:13:11.360
Give me your dagger.

644
01:13:11.360 --> 01:13:58.610
Why don't you just come here?

645
01:13:59.250 --> 01:14:08.700
Did you come from the sea?

646
01:14:10.900 --> 01:14:11.620
Are you alone?

647
01:14:17.490 --> 01:14:19.410
My pigs have taken a liking to you.

648
01:14:20.850 --> 01:14:21.650
Are you hungry?

649
01:14:24.370 --> 01:14:26.050
Leave your weapons at the door.

650
01:14:26.050 --> 01:14:27.970
Enjoy the hospitality of Zeus.

651
01:14:29.970 --> 01:14:30.770
Grab a hog.

652
01:14:30.770 --> 01:14:32.210
You can slaughter it for your dinner.

653
01:14:57.680 --> 01:15:00.080
Whoever you are. you're clearly not a farmer.

654
01:15:00.720 --> 01:15:01.220
Here.

655
01:15:05.170 --> 01:15:06.290
That stew smells good.

656
01:15:10.290 --> 01:15:11.410
That's enough for just us.

657
01:15:13.170 --> 01:15:14.450
Who says it's just us?

658
01:15:16.670 --> 01:15:17.470
Who else is here?

659
01:15:18.190 --> 01:15:18.990
Who indeed?

660
01:15:32.740 --> 01:15:33.940
You say you're all alone.

661
01:15:34.740 --> 01:15:36.180
But no one comes here alone.

662
01:15:37.540 --> 01:15:38.040
No?

663
01:15:39.060 --> 01:15:42.750
You can only reach this spot by sea.

664
01:15:42.750 --> 01:15:43.870
Where are your shipmates?

665
01:15:44.990 --> 01:15:45.870
I'm looking for them.

666
01:15:47.470 --> 01:15:48.830
Anyone happen to come this way?

667
01:15:52.210 --> 01:15:53.810
But you prepared all this food.

668
01:15:53.810 --> 01:15:54.770
You're questioning me?

669
01:15:55.410 --> 01:15:55.910
You?

670
01:15:56.370 --> 01:16:00.660
The traveler who's clearly starving.

671
01:16:00.660 --> 01:16:02.420
It hasn't touched the food in front of him.

672
01:16:03.060 --> 01:16:06.100
Claims to be a sailor. yet wears a great warrior's armor.

673
01:16:07.460 --> 01:16:12.260
And who breaks Zeus's law by bringing a dagger into his hostess's house.

674
01:16:12.900 --> 01:16:13.940
You have sharp eyes.

675
01:16:14.740 --> 01:16:15.940
Sharpened by the gods.

676
01:16:16.900 --> 01:16:18.100
Do they grant you insights?

677
01:16:19.140 --> 01:16:19.700
Truths.

678
01:16:21.460 --> 01:16:22.260
Sometimes.

679
01:16:22.980 --> 01:16:24.900
Would they tell you where I could find my men?

680
01:16:25.940 --> 01:16:27.060
You're a man.

681
01:16:27.060 --> 01:16:27.560
That's right.

682
01:16:28.820 --> 01:16:29.380
They would.

683
01:16:30.020 --> 01:16:30.900
And would you tell me?

684
01:16:31.860 --> 01:16:32.660
Perhaps.

685
01:16:33.380 --> 01:16:36.190
If you give me your dagger.

686
01:16:40.220 --> 01:16:48.380
Why would I help a liar who refuses my hospitality?

687
01:16:49.420 --> 01:17:01.150
I remember here earlier.

688
01:17:01.150 --> 01:17:02.350
Now they're not.

689
01:17:02.350 --> 01:17:09.630
Who. Don?

690
01:17:10.430 --> 01:17:10.930
Yes.

691
01:17:14.380 --> 01:17:15.660
Leaving their rings.

692
01:17:15.660 --> 01:17:29.360
I don't know who this girl is to you.

693
01:17:29.360 --> 01:17:32.800
If you don't put that knife down. I will throttle it to find her.

694
01:17:32.800 --> 01:17:33.920
Don't hurt her.

695
01:17:33.920 --> 01:17:34.420
Family.

696
01:17:36.990 --> 01:17:37.490
Mother.

697
01:17:40.560 --> 01:17:41.120
Your sister.

698
01:17:45.200 --> 01:17:48.000
You did this to her like you turned my men into pigs.

699
01:17:48.080 --> 01:17:49.680
I didn't turn your men into anything.

700
01:17:50.400 --> 01:17:51.280
Look at them.

701
01:17:51.280 --> 01:17:52.240
This is who they are.

702
01:17:52.240 --> 01:17:52.800
Look at them.

703
01:17:53.680 --> 01:17:54.180
Base.

704
01:17:54.800 --> 01:17:55.520
Disgusting.

705
01:17:56.080 --> 01:17:58.720
Primal urges that you value in battle.

706
01:17:58.720 --> 01:18:01.680
They've raped and pillaged across the world for you.

707
01:18:01.680 --> 01:18:03.280
And you think that's against their nature?

708
01:18:03.280 --> 01:18:05.360
You think they only did that because you ordered them to?

709
01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:09.280
A true commander thinks his men's savagery is actually discipline.

710
01:18:10.400 --> 01:18:12.000
And you don't seem that deluded.

711
01:18:12.560 --> 01:18:14.640
But you sent them to me here.

712
01:18:14.640 --> 01:18:15.360
On my own.

713
01:18:16.080 --> 01:18:19.520
A gang of filthy soldiers with empty bellies and hot blood.

714
01:18:21.920 --> 01:18:24.320
You thought they'd respect me and my home?

715
01:18:25.680 --> 01:18:26.320
Look at them.

716
01:18:27.120 --> 01:18:28.080
Know your own men.

717
01:18:31.360 --> 01:18:32.000
They would've.

718
01:18:33.200 --> 01:18:34.240
They just want to go home.

719
01:18:38.420 --> 01:18:38.900
Let them.

720
01:19:38.830 --> 01:19:39.390
Odysseus.

721
01:19:40.590 --> 01:19:41.790
Back to the ship.

722
01:19:41.790 --> 01:19:43.310
Take all the food you can carry.

723
01:19:44.190 --> 01:19:44.910
Leave the meat.

724
01:19:45.550 --> 01:19:46.670
You're not gonna let her live.

725
01:19:46.670 --> 01:19:47.310
She's a witch.

726
01:19:48.270 --> 01:19:49.230
Who gave her powers?

727
01:19:49.870 --> 01:19:50.510
You're gods.

728
01:19:51.230 --> 01:19:52.990
Fill the jugs and skins of the stream.

729
01:19:52.990 --> 01:19:54.270
Be ready to sail at dawn.

730
01:20:00.430 --> 01:20:01.470
Why leave her like this?

731
01:20:02.430 --> 01:20:03.710
We get on better this way.

732
01:20:05.630 --> 01:20:07.070
I'm sorry if we offended you.

733
01:20:08.830 --> 01:20:10.350
We can't get back without your help.

734
01:20:11.150 --> 01:20:12.990
We need to know the way to the trade routes.

735
01:20:12.990 --> 01:20:13.950
You're too lost.

736
01:20:14.750 --> 01:20:15.230
What do you mean?

737
01:20:17.340 --> 01:20:20.300
Only blind Tiresias can tell you how to go home.

738
01:20:20.300 --> 01:20:21.420
Tiresias?

739
01:20:21.420 --> 01:20:22.140
In Hades.

740
01:20:23.020 --> 01:20:25.980
Nobody sails into hell and comes back.

741
01:20:26.620 --> 01:20:27.100
You will.

742
01:20:28.700 --> 01:20:40.960
Head north into the twilight and head inland until a river of fire meets a river of ice.

743
01:21:31.820 --> 01:21:38.430
There you must dig a trench and fill it with the blood of sacrifice.

744
01:21:58.940 --> 01:22:02.940
Gather the dead and learn how you go home.

745
01:22:05.470 --> 01:22:05.790
You.

746
01:22:07.630 --> 01:22:08.430
Not your men.

747
01:22:09.630 --> 01:22:10.510
You'd curse them?

748
01:22:11.390 --> 01:22:12.190
Not a curse.

749
01:22:13.710 --> 01:22:14.350
A truce.

750
01:22:15.870 --> 01:22:18.110
Your men don't want you to defy the gods.

751
01:22:18.750 --> 01:22:22.320
The gods say only you make it home.

752
01:22:23.600 --> 01:22:24.880
Then I defy the gods.

753
01:23:06.770 --> 01:23:07.270
Sinai.

754
01:23:08.370 --> 01:23:09.250
It's Odysseus.

755
01:23:14.670 --> 01:23:15.170
Sinai.

756
01:23:16.590 --> 01:23:17.470
It's Odysseus.

757
01:23:22.900 --> 01:23:24.260
Then you will hear his testimony.

758
01:23:39.140 --> 01:23:39.940
Odysseus?

759
01:23:40.580 --> 01:23:41.080
Yes.

760
01:23:42.100 --> 01:23:43.460
You lied to everyone.

761
01:23:47.500 --> 01:23:48.700
Did you have to lie to me?

762
01:23:50.540 --> 01:23:51.740
I needed you to believe.

763
01:23:53.890 --> 01:23:55.330
Why did you have to volunteer?

764
01:23:56.370 --> 01:23:58.130
Don't you remember how I came here?

765
01:23:58.130 --> 01:24:01.330
I begged to take Antinous's place.

766
01:24:01.330 --> 01:24:02.130
He'd been called.

767
01:24:03.090 --> 01:24:05.890
He offered money for your family if you take his place.

768
01:24:07.090 --> 01:24:12.420
If the Trojans don't let me live. if I never make it home.

769
01:24:13.540 --> 01:24:14.900
take this back to my father.

770
01:24:16.660 --> 01:24:17.700
Tell him everything I did.

771
01:24:21.250 --> 01:24:26.110
Odysseus. feel free to add some stuff.

772
01:24:29.810 --> 01:24:31.650
You didn't trust me with the truth.

773
01:24:33.890 --> 01:24:35.170
You knew I'd die for you.

774
01:24:35.810 --> 01:24:37.250
Dying meant tell the truth.

775
01:24:37.250 --> 01:24:38.290
I needed you to believe.

776
01:24:39.010 --> 01:24:41.330
I died for your lie on that beach.

777
01:24:42.530 --> 01:24:43.030
I know.

778
01:24:43.570 --> 01:24:44.610
I was there with you.

779
01:24:45.250 --> 01:24:48.210
I heard you repeat the lie with your dying breath.

780
01:24:48.210 --> 01:24:49.330
winning us the war.

781
01:24:49.890 --> 01:24:52.690
I was right there. inches from you. smelling your blood.

782
01:24:52.690 --> 01:24:54.290
What comfort could that give me?

783
01:24:56.130 --> 01:24:59.730
Not knowing you were beside that horse until the dead told me.

784
01:25:01.010 --> 01:25:01.570
Which dead?

785
01:25:02.450 --> 01:25:05.410
A lot of veterans of your campaigns walk these shores.

786
01:25:08.110 --> 01:25:12.670
The dead meet to lament the living and greet the newly dead.

787
01:25:13.710 --> 01:25:15.950
My father was the first shade to find me.

788
01:25:16.590 --> 01:25:17.310
To comfort you.

789
01:25:18.110 --> 01:25:19.310
Shades don't comfort.

790
01:25:20.190 --> 01:25:23.470
They want word of the living who wronged them.

791
01:25:24.350 --> 01:25:27.710
My father had died in poverty. alone.

792
01:25:28.430 --> 01:25:31.390
begging for scraps from people like Antinous.

793
01:25:32.030 --> 01:25:33.310
Antinous cheated you?

794
01:25:34.350 --> 01:25:36.030
Aziz no doubt cheating you.

795
01:25:36.750 --> 01:25:37.470
How?

796
01:25:37.470 --> 01:25:40.430
Who's in Ithaca looking after your wife and son?

797
01:25:40.430 --> 01:25:41.790
My wife is of no concern.

798
01:25:43.710 --> 01:25:45.950
Talk to Agamemnon about homecomings.

799
01:25:47.070 --> 01:25:48.110
Agamemnon's here?

800
01:25:51.620 --> 01:25:54.100
You buried me at Troy with respect.

801
01:25:54.100 --> 01:25:54.580
Thank you.

802
01:25:55.620 --> 01:25:57.460
But spare a thought for those you didn't.

803
01:25:58.180 --> 01:25:59.620
It's not always possible.

804
01:26:00.740 --> 01:26:01.540
When you get home.

805
01:26:01.860 --> 01:26:07.220
take a ship. head into the unknown west till you find land.

806
01:26:07.220 --> 01:26:10.660
And there. give sacrifice and honor your men.

807
01:26:13.250 --> 01:26:14.450
Do you still have the lot?

808
01:26:18.400 --> 01:26:18.880
Of course.

809
01:26:20.080 --> 01:26:23.520
If you make it home. give Antinous back his shame.

810
01:26:23.520 --> 01:26:36.260
I will. my lord.

811
01:26:48.400 --> 01:26:51.200
Odysseus. how are you here?

812
01:26:52.240 --> 01:26:53.840
We won the war. you're headed for home.

813
01:26:54.720 --> 01:26:58.000
Odysseus. the most brilliant of all my generals.

814
01:26:59.600 --> 01:27:01.360
You would never be snared as I was.

815
01:27:01.360 --> 01:27:03.040
When you cast a drift. like us.

816
01:27:04.160 --> 01:27:06.640
No. my misfortune was to make it home.

817
01:27:08.160 --> 01:27:10.000
To be welcomed by my beautiful wife.

818
01:27:16.510 --> 01:27:24.290
bathed. anointed. made soft.

819
01:27:25.970 --> 01:27:27.250
to learn the hardest lesson.

820
01:27:28.370 --> 01:27:30.530
I'd left a poison in her heart years before.

821
01:27:33.570 --> 01:27:34.690
Good part of her heart had gone.

822
01:27:41.600 --> 01:27:43.920
She and her lover had plotted my murder for years.

823
01:27:45.760 --> 01:27:46.480
Odysseus.

824
01:27:46.480 --> 01:27:48.240
Odysseus. yes.

825
01:27:49.360 --> 01:27:52.080
My son Orestes. did he avenge me?

826
01:27:52.720 --> 01:27:53.760
My lord. how could I know?

827
01:27:55.200 --> 01:27:56.400
I'm still trying to get home.

828
01:27:57.920 --> 01:27:58.960
Don't make my mistake.

829
01:28:00.560 --> 01:28:03.280
Don't walk in the front door expecting garlands and praise.

830
01:28:04.400 --> 01:28:05.280
Come in disguise.

831
01:28:06.480 --> 01:28:07.200
Take your time.

832
01:28:08.480 --> 01:28:20.660
Odysseus. I know what I have to do.

833
01:28:21.380 --> 01:28:23.140
Some of what you have to do.

834
01:28:23.140 --> 01:28:24.980
My prophecy is not for their ears.

835
01:28:26.820 --> 01:28:27.540
Back to the ship.

836
01:28:30.180 --> 01:28:32.500
Sail southwest past the sirens.

837
01:28:34.020 --> 01:28:36.500
Their song lures your men onto the rocks.

838
01:28:36.500 --> 01:28:37.540
They all die.

839
01:28:37.540 --> 01:28:38.580
I won't let that happen.

840
01:28:39.460 --> 01:28:41.380
I'll block their ears with wax.

841
01:28:42.020 --> 01:28:43.300
Past the sirens.

842
01:28:43.300 --> 01:28:47.380
you choose between the whirlpool Charybdis and the monster Scylla.

843
01:28:47.380 --> 01:28:50.020
The whirlpool in the sea takes all of your lives.

844
01:28:50.020 --> 01:28:52.020
The monster in the cliffs. only six.

845
01:28:52.580 --> 01:28:55.860
But your determination to not sacrifice any more lives

846
01:28:55.860 --> 01:28:56.740
kills all of them.

847
01:28:57.700 --> 01:28:59.060
I'll do what I have to.

848
01:28:59.060 --> 01:29:01.860
At the end. when starvation and mutiny threaten.

849
01:29:01.860 --> 01:29:05.220
they demand to land where the sun god grazes his cattle.

850
01:29:05.780 --> 01:29:07.380
They slaughter Apollo's herd.

851
01:29:09.060 --> 01:29:10.340
Zeus kills them for it.

852
01:29:11.060 --> 01:29:11.620
Do you see?

853
01:29:13.140 --> 01:29:13.860
I'll stop them.

854
01:29:14.500 --> 01:29:16.260
Why do you value these men's lives

855
01:29:16.260 --> 01:29:18.020
more than those you've already lost?

856
01:29:18.020 --> 01:29:19.780
I can still save them from the gods.

857
01:29:21.220 --> 01:29:22.900
But not from themselves.

858
01:29:25.550 --> 01:29:26.590
What is that?

859
01:29:26.590 --> 01:29:34.110
Your own barrier. Dishonor.

860
01:29:34.190 --> 01:30:54.940
So many fallen comrade.

861
01:30:54.940 --> 01:30:56.140
I have to save the rest.

862
01:30:57.340 --> 01:30:58.300
What's happened to them?

863
01:30:59.260 --> 01:31:00.460
Did they leave me here?

864
01:31:01.020 --> 01:31:01.980
Are they coming back?

865
01:31:04.500 --> 01:31:05.940
Did you do something to them?

866
01:31:07.780 --> 01:31:09.140
Did you do something to them?

867
01:31:11.010 --> 01:31:13.410
You told me not to eat the lotus flower.

868
01:31:13.410 --> 01:31:20.130
Just tonight so you can get some sleep.

869
01:31:20.130 --> 01:31:22.130
And tomorrow when you're ready.

870
01:31:22.130 --> 01:31:30.270
you'll remember for your family.

871
01:31:30.270 --> 01:31:30.990
Family?

872
01:31:30.990 --> 01:31:37.140
Do I have a wife?

873
01:31:37.620 --> 01:31:44.300
It's all right. Melantho.

874
01:31:45.180 --> 01:31:46.220
Strangers don't come in here.

875
01:31:46.860 --> 01:31:48.140
Scream or no scream.

876
01:31:48.140 --> 01:31:49.580
after three years of feasting together.

877
01:31:49.580 --> 01:31:51.340
we're far from strangers. Melody.

878
01:31:51.340 --> 01:31:54.780
Are you trying to marry me or ruin my reputation?

879
01:31:54.780 --> 01:31:56.460
Melantho can be discreet.

880
01:31:56.460 --> 01:32:00.140
I'm actually here out of concern for your reputation.

881
01:32:00.140 --> 01:32:00.540
Oh?

882
01:32:01.180 --> 01:32:02.780
Everyone sees how hard you work.

883
01:32:02.780 --> 01:32:05.260
Slow. steady progress.

884
01:32:06.620 --> 01:32:08.780
But every night you've been unraveling that work.

885
01:32:17.760 --> 01:32:20.480
You can't expect her to be loyal to her master.

886
01:32:20.480 --> 01:32:21.600
She never even knew.

887
01:32:21.600 --> 01:32:23.520
I expect her to be loyal to me.

888
01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:24.720
Then she will be.

889
01:32:24.720 --> 01:32:25.600
And you're married to me.

890
01:32:27.280 --> 01:32:30.400
Slaves have no choice but to be loyal to the future.

891
01:32:30.960 --> 01:32:33.360
This is a household waiting for a master.

892
01:32:33.360 --> 01:32:35.360
just as Ethika is a kingdom waiting for a king.

893
01:32:38.140 --> 01:32:39.580
Ethika's king is coming back.

894
01:32:40.380 --> 01:32:41.020
No. he's not.

895
01:32:43.140 --> 01:32:44.660
Do you know that as a boy.

896
01:32:44.660 --> 01:32:45.860
I tried to go with him to Troy?

897
01:32:47.220 --> 01:32:48.180
He never told me that.

898
01:32:50.020 --> 01:32:51.540
Desi's refused to let me.

899
01:32:51.540 --> 01:33:01.460
He knew that Ethika needed a future without him.

900
01:33:01.460 --> 01:33:03.220
And he saw me as part of that future.

901
01:33:08.290 --> 01:33:13.170
I'll never tell other suitors about your deception.

902
01:33:13.170 --> 01:33:17.360
Finish your weaving.

903
01:33:17.360 --> 01:33:32.180
Why would you want to marry someone who loves someone else?

904
01:33:32.740 --> 01:33:39.940
Your loyalty to Odysseus is one of your finest qualities.

905
01:33:43.660 --> 01:33:44.460
I love you.

906
01:33:45.660 --> 01:33:48.460
I want you to choose me and end all this.

907
01:33:51.020 --> 01:33:52.460
Tell me you're not tired.

908
01:33:53.420 --> 01:34:01.010
The waiting. the uncertainty. your duty.

909
01:34:27.380 --> 01:34:28.820
I'll take care of all your needs.

910
01:34:37.790 --> 01:34:40.560
And my son?

911
01:34:40.560 --> 01:34:41.520
The throne is his.

912
01:34:42.400 --> 01:34:58.060
He's the heir. unless we have a child of our own.

913
01:34:58.060 --> 01:34:59.020
Where is Telemachus?

914
01:35:01.140 --> 01:35:03.940
He's gone inland to visit his grandfather.

915
01:35:29.790 --> 01:35:31.150
He's gone from here to his father.

916
01:35:32.350 --> 01:35:32.670
Where?

917
01:35:33.470 --> 01:35:35.550
He's gone to Sparta. to Menelaus.

918
01:35:37.070 --> 01:35:37.950
Well. should we go there?

919
01:35:39.710 --> 01:35:40.210
No.

920
01:35:42.110 --> 01:35:43.310
Move out. Melanthius.

921
01:35:43.310 --> 01:35:44.750
I told you you're a coward.

922
01:35:46.270 --> 01:35:47.150
He was listening.

923
01:35:47.150 --> 01:35:48.750
No. no. no. no.

924
01:35:49.390 --> 01:35:52.430
I was coming for Argus.

925
01:35:53.870 --> 01:35:55.470
I told you that dog stays there.

926
01:35:55.470 --> 01:35:56.590
Go back to the farm.

927
01:35:56.590 --> 01:35:58.270
When we need your pigs. we'll send Melanthius.

928
01:35:59.550 --> 01:36:01.950
You're no longer welcome in this palace. old man.

929
01:36:01.950 --> 01:36:03.550
I answer to my mistress.

930
01:36:06.210 --> 01:36:07.250
Does she even know your name?

931
01:36:09.010 --> 01:36:09.510
Now go.

932
01:36:10.690 --> 01:36:14.050
Telemachus will have to come back by the temple at Pylos.

933
01:36:14.690 --> 01:36:15.970
You take your man away from there.

934
01:36:16.850 --> 01:36:19.010
Well. he can stay and try and charm the queen.

935
01:36:19.010 --> 01:36:19.730
I don't think so.

936
01:36:20.930 --> 01:36:23.890
He needs to die on that road. and Iris has a plan.

937
01:36:25.250 --> 01:36:25.750
Iris!

938
01:36:28.420 --> 01:36:32.740
I need everyone in his party dead in a ditch. throats open.

939
01:36:32.740 --> 01:36:35.060
Telemachus and his men will be ready for the bandits

940
01:36:35.060 --> 01:36:36.980
who work the road between here and Pylos.

941
01:36:38.260 --> 01:36:40.580
But if some of them took the temple from the priests

942
01:36:40.580 --> 01:36:44.900
before they arrived. then bring me part of Telemachus' proof.

943
01:36:46.820 --> 01:36:47.380
His nose.

944
01:36:48.420 --> 01:36:49.940
Would you know him only by his nose?

945
01:36:53.790 --> 01:36:54.510
His ears. too.

946
01:37:00.380 --> 01:37:01.020
Who taught you?

947
01:37:01.660 --> 01:37:02.860
My father's swineherd.

948
01:37:03.500 --> 01:37:04.140
Swineherd?

949
01:37:04.860 --> 01:37:05.980
As most loyal servant.

950
01:37:06.940 --> 01:37:09.900
Odysseus left his most loyal servant behind.

951
01:37:09.900 --> 01:37:11.340
He always was the smartest.

952
01:37:12.460 --> 01:37:14.300
Only one Agamemnon ever listened to.

953
01:37:15.660 --> 01:37:16.700
Should have listened harder.

954
01:37:17.580 --> 01:37:19.340
Can I ask what happened to your brother?

955
01:37:19.340 --> 01:37:21.420
His wife betrayed him and murdered him.

956
01:37:22.540 --> 01:37:23.580
What did you do to him?

957
01:37:23.580 --> 01:37:24.780
We sent arrestees.

958
01:37:24.780 --> 01:37:25.580
He killed them both.

959
01:37:26.460 --> 01:37:27.740
But he killed his own mother?

960
01:37:29.180 --> 01:37:30.300
He avenged his father.

961
01:37:31.180 --> 01:37:32.700
But you don't have to worry.

962
01:37:32.700 --> 01:37:35.180
Your mother has nothing in common with Clytemestra.

963
01:37:35.740 --> 01:37:36.380
Maybe not.

964
01:37:36.940 --> 01:37:39.900
But the suitors have convinced the elders it's time she remarried.

965
01:37:39.900 --> 01:37:40.700
Yeah.

966
01:37:40.700 --> 01:37:42.460
It's not a good time for an empty throne.

967
01:37:43.260 --> 01:37:44.380
I've heard the stories.

968
01:37:44.380 --> 01:37:45.180
People from the sea.

969
01:37:45.820 --> 01:37:46.780
Have you been raided?

970
01:37:46.780 --> 01:37:49.580
Not yet. but the attacks. they do seem closer.

971
01:37:49.580 --> 01:37:50.860
Sparta's ready.

972
01:37:50.860 --> 01:37:51.660
What about Ithaca?

973
01:37:52.460 --> 01:37:54.860
She'd be able to raise an army. defend herself.

974
01:37:54.860 --> 01:37:56.380
Someone's got to take the throne.

975
01:37:56.380 --> 01:37:58.460
Then the battle is there. not here. son.

976
01:37:59.500 --> 01:38:00.780
You've run from the problem.

977
01:38:02.140 --> 01:38:04.220
Left your mother. the mercy of the suitors.

978
01:38:05.820 --> 01:38:07.180
Should I hire mercenaries?

979
01:38:07.820 --> 01:38:08.940
Go back and kill them all?

980
01:38:10.060 --> 01:38:12.300
Murder rich and powerful guests in your own home.

981
01:38:12.940 --> 01:38:13.820
You'd be exiled.

982
01:38:15.020 --> 01:38:15.500
What then?

983
01:38:16.060 --> 01:38:18.620
You must evict them without violating Zeus's law.

984
01:38:21.100 --> 01:38:22.380
I have to take the throne.

985
01:38:23.740 --> 01:38:25.420
I have to know if Odysseus is alive.

986
01:38:26.300 --> 01:38:28.620
This has nothing to do with a father you've never known.

987
01:38:29.740 --> 01:38:33.100
You have to lead. son. no matter what's happened to Odysseus.

988
01:38:47.470 --> 01:38:49.070
You need to remember.

989
01:38:49.070 --> 01:38:49.710
No. I don't.

990
01:38:50.990 --> 01:38:52.270
I don't need to know the truth.

991
01:38:52.270 --> 01:38:53.550
I'm happy here with you.

992
01:38:56.320 --> 01:38:59.920
Why are you gathering the bones of your old ship to make a raft?

993
01:39:02.610 --> 01:39:03.570
Remember your crew.

994
01:39:05.570 --> 01:39:06.690
Your journey.

995
01:39:06.690 --> 01:39:12.640
I heard the sound of the sirens.

996
01:39:22.580 --> 01:39:24.100
Put the wax in the men's ears.

997
01:39:25.380 --> 01:39:26.180
None for me.

998
01:39:26.180 --> 01:39:27.540
Tie me to the mast.

999
01:39:27.540 --> 01:39:28.260
Why?

1000
01:39:28.260 --> 01:39:30.180
I want to hear the siren's song.

1001
01:39:30.180 --> 01:39:30.680
No.

1002
01:39:31.460 --> 01:39:32.340
That's a bad idea.

1003
01:39:33.060 --> 01:39:34.580
No one's ever heard the song and lived.

1004
01:39:34.580 --> 01:39:35.780
That's why it's a good idea.

1005
01:39:35.780 --> 01:39:36.580
I'll be the first.

1006
01:39:37.860 --> 01:39:41.780
No matter how hard I plead. do not untie me until we're well clear.

1007
01:39:43.220 --> 01:39:44.100
Check the men's ears.

1008
01:39:45.300 --> 01:39:47.060
Awake's rations for the first.

1009
01:39:47.060 --> 01:39:47.700
Yes. sir.

1010
01:39:48.260 --> 01:40:47.760
What was the siren's song?

1011
01:40:48.720 --> 01:40:50.720
You know all the things you want it to be.

1012
01:40:50.720 --> 01:40:55.840
The things you wish you never wished for.

1013
01:40:55.840 --> 01:41:16.450
A delicious itch. you go to scratch.

1014
01:41:18.130 --> 01:41:20.450
To find it's under the skin. you can't reach it.

1015
01:41:23.220 --> 01:41:25.700
So the delicious itch becomes unbearable.

1016
01:41:25.700 --> 01:41:41.220
Told you what you most want is what you most. you most can't have.

1017
01:41:41.220 --> 01:42:14.060
All the promises I failed to keep told me I don't really want to go home.

1018
01:42:14.060 --> 01:42:14.700
Charybdis.

1019
01:42:16.620 --> 01:42:17.980
A monstrous whirlpool.

1020
01:42:20.960 --> 01:42:21.760
Hoist the sails.

1021
01:42:21.760 --> 01:42:23.360
Come on. let's go.

1022
01:42:23.440 --> 01:42:26.980
We head out to sea.

1023
01:42:26.980 --> 01:42:27.940
It's already got us.

1024
01:42:35.120 --> 01:42:36.320
We might make that canyon.

1025
01:42:36.320 --> 01:42:37.120
It's too narrow.

1026
01:42:37.760 --> 01:42:39.280
Make for the whirlpool.

1027
01:42:39.280 --> 01:42:43.360
If you steer down the slope. we can make enough way to shoot out the other side.

1028
01:42:43.360 --> 01:42:44.160
Don't be afraid.

1029
01:42:44.160 --> 01:42:45.040
We can do it.

1030
01:42:45.040 --> 01:42:53.340
We're making for the channel.

1031
01:42:54.220 --> 01:42:55.820
We're making for the channel.

1032
01:42:55.820 --> 01:45:07.540
If I told you. you wouldn't have chosen this way.

1033
01:45:07.540 --> 01:45:09.860
And those men would still be alive.

1034
01:45:09.860 --> 01:45:11.460
No. we'd all be dead.

1035
01:45:12.020 --> 01:45:13.300
You had no right.

1036
01:45:13.300 --> 01:45:16.900
I had every right and every responsibility.

1037
01:45:16.900 --> 01:45:19.940
The whirlpool would have destroyed the ship and all of us.

1038
01:45:19.940 --> 01:45:21.140
This way we live.

1039
01:45:21.140 --> 01:45:22.420
Some of us live.

1040
01:45:22.420 --> 01:45:22.740
Yes.

1041
01:45:26.800 --> 01:45:28.800
What else did Tiresias tell you?

1042
01:45:28.800 --> 01:45:41.740
I'm gonna put in there.

1043
01:45:41.740 --> 01:45:45.760
There.

1044
01:45:45.760 --> 01:45:47.600
That's the Sun God's Isle.

1045
01:45:47.600 --> 01:45:51.980
We men are exhausted.

1046
01:45:51.980 --> 01:45:53.820
I'm on six shipments.

1047
01:45:53.820 --> 01:45:56.860
You're gonna put in. we're gonna rest up on dry land.

1048
01:45:56.860 --> 01:45:57.900
You're all gonna die.

1049
01:46:01.120 --> 01:46:03.040
You asked me what Tiresias told me.

1050
01:46:04.160 --> 01:46:05.440
He said you're all gonna die.

1051
01:46:06.400 --> 01:46:12.400
If you slaughter the Sun God's cattle. which graze on that island. you'll all die.

1052
01:46:17.060 --> 01:46:17.700
That's fine.

1053
01:46:18.980 --> 01:46:19.620
We'll put in.

1054
01:46:20.820 --> 01:46:22.420
We'll cook our own provisions.

1055
01:46:22.420 --> 01:46:23.220
We'll pick fruit.

1056
01:46:23.220 --> 01:46:24.340
We'll get water.

1057
01:46:24.340 --> 01:46:25.060
Nothing more.

1058
01:46:26.660 --> 01:46:27.460
Swear to it.

1059
01:46:28.500 --> 01:46:29.060
All of you.

1060
01:46:43.540 --> 01:46:43.860
See?

1061
01:46:45.380 --> 01:46:46.660
The wind took us right in.

1062
01:46:48.100 --> 01:46:51.060
We'll rest up. do the repairs.

1063
01:46:51.060 --> 01:46:52.740
We'll set out as soon as the wind changes.

1064
01:46:53.940 --> 01:46:54.820
Grab that line.

1065
01:47:06.260 --> 01:47:07.780
Did the crew keep their promise?

1066
01:47:09.220 --> 01:47:09.540
Yes.

1067
01:47:10.980 --> 01:47:11.940
For a long time.

1068
01:47:13.980 --> 01:47:15.740
But the wind never changed.

1069
01:47:15.740 --> 01:47:19.260
The steady onshore breeze that made it so easy for us to land

1070
01:47:22.140 --> 01:47:29.820
made it impossible to leave. and our provisions ran out.

1071
01:47:29.820 --> 01:48:39.250
Did you think if you ate quietly the Sun God wouldn't notice?

1072
01:48:39.970 --> 01:48:41.170
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1073
01:48:42.370 --> 01:48:43.810
I'm not the problem.

1074
01:48:43.810 --> 01:48:44.770
I wanted you to live.

1075
01:48:50.910 --> 01:48:51.950
Tiresias told you.

1076
01:48:52.430 --> 01:48:53.230
Circe told you.

1077
01:48:53.230 --> 01:48:54.190
The gods told you.

1078
01:48:55.390 --> 01:48:57.870
You gods don't speak in ways we understand.

1079
01:48:57.870 --> 01:49:01.470
Who doesn't understand pain or blood or death?

1080
01:49:02.670 --> 01:49:05.470
When you watch your men die. do you see chance or do you see consequence?

1081
01:49:06.270 --> 01:49:07.630
I see consequence.

1082
01:49:07.630 --> 01:49:08.750
I see my failure.

1083
01:49:09.470 --> 01:49:11.470
What if their deaths aren't your failure. but theirs?

1084
01:49:12.270 --> 01:49:12.990
I lead them.

1085
01:49:14.910 --> 01:49:15.790
I led them here.

1086
01:49:16.510 --> 01:49:17.390
And who led you?

1087
01:49:19.070 --> 01:49:20.670
Why do you put yourself above the gods?

1088
01:49:20.670 --> 01:49:22.430
Why do you claim responsibility?

1089
01:49:23.550 --> 01:49:24.750
Why don't you want to go home?

1090
01:49:28.380 --> 01:49:29.580
They ran out of food.

1091
01:49:31.020 --> 01:49:32.060
Have some pity.

1092
01:49:32.060 --> 01:49:35.100
My pity is what put us on this damn island.

1093
01:49:35.100 --> 01:49:36.540
I told you what would happen.

1094
01:49:36.540 --> 01:49:37.180
Yes. you did.

1095
01:49:38.220 --> 01:49:40.860
And we decided we would rather drown than starve.

1096
01:49:46.880 --> 01:49:49.200
Well. now's our chance.

1097
01:49:49.200 --> 01:49:50.560
The wind has dropped!

1098
01:49:50.560 --> 01:49:51.600
The wind has dropped!

1099
01:49:51.600 --> 01:49:58.880
The calm was just long enough to lure us out onto Poseidon's ocean.

1100
01:50:00.480 --> 01:50:01.840
under Zeus' sky.

1101
01:50:01.840 --> 01:51:57.010
It was the harsh hand of Zeus.

1102
01:51:57.010 --> 01:52:48.130
Exactly what you told them would happen.

1103
01:52:48.130 --> 01:52:55.810
Washed up on my shore with some of your boat and none of your crew.

1104
01:52:55.810 --> 01:53:28.050
Left dead. unconscious for days and two weeks.

1105
01:53:29.170 --> 01:53:32.050
I knew the lotus would ease your awakening

1106
01:53:32.050 --> 01:53:35.860
so that your pain was more of the body than the mind.

1107
01:53:36.420 --> 01:53:40.100
And it did.

1108
01:53:40.100 --> 01:53:52.430
So willingly cared for you.

1109
01:53:54.640 --> 01:53:56.000
I came to love you.

1110
01:53:56.000 --> 01:54:08.030
Kept telling myself to stop giving it to you.

1111
01:54:08.030 --> 01:54:09.950
That you were strong enough to remember.

1112
01:54:09.950 --> 01:54:11.070
How long. Cullep's honey?

1113
01:54:11.070 --> 01:54:12.350
You were so happy.

1114
01:54:12.350 --> 01:54:13.230
You were happy.

1115
01:54:13.230 --> 01:54:15.150
We were so happy.

1116
01:54:15.150 --> 01:54:15.790
For years.

1117
01:54:16.430 --> 01:54:16.830
Years?

1118
01:54:24.130 --> 01:54:24.850
Seven years.

1119
01:54:26.130 --> 01:54:29.890
You fed me lotus flower and held me captive.

1120
01:54:29.890 --> 01:54:31.090
I saved your life.

1121
01:54:32.210 --> 01:54:33.410
I rebuilt your body.

1122
01:54:33.410 --> 01:54:36.130
I just couldn't bring myself to rebuild your mind.

1123
01:54:37.810 --> 01:54:38.850
You have been happy.

1124
01:54:40.050 --> 01:54:42.850
Your heart was just always somewhere else.

1125
01:54:44.530 --> 01:54:47.410
And your mind was bound to follow eventually.

1126
01:54:48.850 --> 01:54:49.570
How could you?

1127
01:54:50.210 --> 01:54:53.490
The lotus clouds the mind and saps the will.

1128
01:54:53.490 --> 01:54:55.330
but it doesn't change who you are.

1129
01:54:56.130 --> 01:54:58.930
You weren't ready to go home for whatever reason.

1130
01:54:59.490 --> 01:55:00.290
I am now.

1131
01:55:02.210 --> 01:55:03.650
As I always knew you would be.

1132
01:55:04.770 --> 01:55:05.650
How?

1133
01:55:05.650 --> 01:55:08.530
You were clutching something when I found you.

1134
01:55:08.530 --> 01:55:10.210
Your hand wouldn't open for weeks.

1135
01:55:11.250 --> 01:55:12.130
You'd call out.

1136
01:55:13.250 --> 01:55:14.450
Mostly to Athena.

1137
01:55:14.450 --> 01:55:17.170
But sometimes you would say another name.

1138
01:55:17.810 --> 01:55:32.210
Help me.

1139
01:55:37.040 --> 01:55:37.920
Help me go home.

1140
01:55:39.120 --> 01:55:39.680
I am.

1141
01:55:41.360 --> 01:55:43.440
You're a man who needs to control his fate.

1142
01:55:45.120 --> 01:55:46.480
But you cannot control this.

1143
01:55:50.190 --> 01:55:51.950
You must give yourself to the storm.

1144
01:55:52.910 --> 01:55:54.430
Give yourself to Poseidon.

1145
01:55:54.910 --> 01:55:56.430
And take your punishment.

1146
01:55:57.070 --> 01:56:00.990
Zeus won't let him kill you.

1147
01:56:01.630 --> 01:56:05.860
Give up the fight.

1148
01:56:05.860 --> 01:56:07.860
Give up control and live.

1149
01:56:08.740 --> 01:56:14.820
It's a leap of faith you've never yet made. Odysseus.

1150
01:56:15.380 --> 01:56:16.020
Odysseus.

1151
01:57:08.430 --> 01:57:17.040
Where am I?

1152
01:57:17.680 --> 01:57:20.850
Don't you recognize your own home?

1153
01:57:21.570 --> 01:58:02.140
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1154
01:58:03.500 --> 01:58:04.380
How many are you?

1155
01:58:05.100 --> 01:58:05.900
Half a dozen.

1156
01:58:06.620 --> 01:58:07.500
Need of a meal?

1157
01:58:08.060 --> 01:58:08.860
And supplies.

1158
01:58:09.820 --> 01:58:10.620
For the road.

1159
01:58:11.260 --> 01:58:12.140
Where you headed?

1160
01:58:13.340 --> 01:58:14.700
We need pigs.

1161
01:58:14.700 --> 01:58:16.220
We're going all the way to Pylos.

1162
01:58:16.860 --> 01:58:17.740
Are you pilgrims?

1163
01:58:19.020 --> 01:58:20.780
Do we look like pilgrims?

1164
01:58:20.780 --> 01:58:21.660
I don't see well.

1165
01:58:23.020 --> 01:58:26.940
Zeus demands I offer you a meal. but these pigs aren't mine to sell.

1166
01:58:26.940 --> 01:58:27.660
Oh. don't worry.

1167
01:58:28.700 --> 01:58:29.340
We're not paying.

1168
01:58:33.230 --> 01:58:50.500
Give my greetings to your mother.

1169
01:58:50.500 --> 01:58:52.740
Sir. I had no news of your father.

1170
01:58:52.740 --> 01:58:53.700
No. you had news.

1171
01:58:54.340 --> 01:58:56.020
Not of where he is. but who he is.

1172
01:58:57.700 --> 01:58:59.860
And now more than ever I hope to one day meet him.

1173
01:59:00.260 --> 01:59:07.550
Where are your watchdogs. old man?

1174
01:59:07.550 --> 01:59:13.340
I'm burning their bodies.

1175
01:59:18.350 --> 01:59:20.750
Can Zeus offer you food. Drake stranger?

1176
01:59:21.870 --> 01:59:22.670
Were you attacked?

1177
01:59:23.710 --> 01:59:25.630
The dog saved your farmhands.

1178
01:59:25.630 --> 01:59:27.630
Oh. he burned his brothers first.

1179
01:59:30.910 --> 01:59:32.110
What's happened to Ithaca?

1180
01:59:32.990 --> 01:59:33.630
Darkness.

1181
01:59:34.270 --> 01:59:36.510
Zeus's laws smashed to pieces.

1182
01:59:37.550 --> 01:59:41.070
A kingdom without a king since my master died.

1183
01:59:41.070 --> 01:59:41.710
Your master?

1184
01:59:42.830 --> 01:59:43.710
Odysseus.

1185
01:59:43.710 --> 01:59:44.210
Yes.

1186
01:59:44.990 --> 01:59:46.670
Hero of the Trojan War.

1187
01:59:46.670 --> 01:59:50.100
But Odysseus is alive.

1188
01:59:52.660 --> 01:59:55.620
No more. no more. no more!

1189
01:59:55.620 --> 01:59:58.980
No more. no more. no more. no more!

1190
01:59:58.980 --> 02:00:00.260
Telemachus.

1191
02:00:00.260 --> 02:00:01.380
Telemachus. Telemachus.

1192
02:00:02.100 --> 02:00:02.740
Telemachus.

1193
02:00:04.260 --> 02:00:06.580
He's in danger and I can't help him.

1194
02:00:06.580 --> 02:00:10.260
There must be others in Ithaca that would help Odysseus's son.

1195
02:00:10.260 --> 02:00:12.260
Palus is controlled by Sudeis.

1196
02:00:13.220 --> 02:00:15.380
No one's still loyal to Odysseus.

1197
02:00:16.820 --> 02:00:17.780
What about the queen?

1198
02:00:18.900 --> 02:00:20.980
Beautiful. wise Penelope.

1199
02:00:21.940 --> 02:00:24.660
Held hostage by the worst men.

1200
02:00:24.660 --> 02:00:26.660
Soon to be forced into marriage.

1201
02:00:26.660 --> 02:00:29.220
What could she do without even her son?

1202
02:00:29.220 --> 02:00:30.500
Here. drink.

1203
02:00:30.500 --> 02:00:36.930
What's the danger to Telemachus?

1204
02:00:37.890 --> 02:00:39.810
He's on his way back from Sparta.

1205
02:00:41.250 --> 02:00:45.650
The Sudeis sent Pantheon to Palus to ambush him at the temple.

1206
02:00:46.690 --> 02:00:47.890
Give me food for the road.

1207
02:00:48.690 --> 02:00:51.810
You'll never catch up with the bandits.

1208
02:00:51.810 --> 02:00:54.850
If I can get to Palus before Telemachus arrives. I can warn him.

1209
02:00:55.490 --> 02:00:56.770
Why are you helping?

1210
02:00:57.890 --> 02:00:59.490
If I tell you. don't be angry.

1211
02:01:00.290 --> 02:01:02.370
I served with Odysseus at Troy.

1212
02:01:02.370 --> 02:01:02.870
Really?

1213
02:01:04.530 --> 02:01:05.970
He was a harsh taskmaster.

1214
02:01:05.970 --> 02:01:07.010
Oh. but fair.

1215
02:01:08.850 --> 02:01:10.770
Fair and worthy of following.

1216
02:01:12.290 --> 02:01:12.930
You loved him.

1217
02:01:14.130 --> 02:01:14.930
Like a son.

1218
02:01:17.470 --> 02:01:18.350
He's not dead.

1219
02:01:18.990 --> 02:01:20.510
Is she coming home?

1220
02:01:21.870 --> 02:01:22.370
Soon.

1221
02:01:23.230 --> 02:01:24.270
Bringing events.

1222
02:01:27.020 --> 02:01:37.780
Bringing it all.

1223
02:01:37.780 --> 02:01:38.660
Well talked.

1224
02:01:38.660 --> 02:01:40.660
You don't want people realizing who you are.

1225
02:01:40.660 --> 02:01:42.340
Well. maybe we shouldn't have brought a guard.

1226
02:01:42.900 --> 02:01:44.980
We'll be glad we did if we have trouble on the road.

1227
02:02:05.310 --> 02:02:06.110
Find that beggar.

1228
02:02:22.610 --> 02:02:24.130
Your men must wait out here.

1229
02:02:24.130 --> 02:02:24.850
The weapons. too.

1230
02:02:42.240 --> 02:02:43.680
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1231
02:02:44.320 --> 02:02:45.360
Have you traveled far?

1232
02:02:46.080 --> 02:02:46.880
Not that far.

1233
02:02:48.480 --> 02:02:50.080
You're here to rest for the night.

1234
02:02:52.510 --> 02:02:54.430
We're here to make sacrifice to Athena.

1235
02:02:56.780 --> 02:03:00.060
And whatever hospitality you can offer might be considered.

1236
02:03:00.060 --> 02:03:00.540
Of course.

1237
02:03:01.100 --> 02:03:02.300
Get that beggar out of here.

1238
02:03:02.300 --> 02:03:03.580
He has a message for me.

1239
02:03:04.940 --> 02:03:05.900
He may have sent you.

1240
02:03:07.420 --> 02:03:14.260
Yes. sir.

1241
02:03:14.260 --> 02:03:15.220
Well. what's the message?

1242
02:03:16.980 --> 02:03:17.700
It's private.

1243
02:03:18.260 --> 02:03:19.060
These men are pr-

1244
02:03:21.060 --> 02:03:21.940
These men are pr-

1245
02:03:39.820 --> 02:03:41.660
This is your moment to leave. old man.

1246
02:03:42.620 --> 02:03:43.500
Go. stranger.

1247
02:03:43.980 --> 02:03:48.130
I already spared one old man this week.

1248
02:03:48.850 --> 02:03:49.810
But he was blind.

1249
02:03:50.690 --> 02:03:51.490
I won't spare-

1250
02:03:57.790 --> 02:04:31.900
I'm for a beggar.

1251
02:04:32.860 --> 02:04:33.980
No one's born a beggar.

1252
02:04:34.860 --> 02:04:36.060
We'll take this priest's robes.

1253
02:04:36.620 --> 02:04:37.660
Put him over your armor.

1254
02:04:40.060 --> 02:04:41.340
Help me with his body.

1255
02:04:41.340 --> 02:04:42.220
We have to leave him.

1256
02:04:43.020 --> 02:04:43.420
No.

1257
02:04:43.420 --> 02:04:44.540
We have to slip out.

1258
02:04:45.420 --> 02:04:46.940
That's not how you honor the dead.

1259
02:04:46.940 --> 02:04:48.300
That is how you stay alive.

1260
02:04:48.300 --> 02:04:49.340
He was my teacher.

1261
02:04:50.780 --> 02:04:52.380
He might have been Athena in disguise-

1262
02:04:52.380 --> 02:04:53.500
His name was Mentor.

1263
02:04:54.300 --> 02:04:55.580
And when you are safe.

1264
02:04:55.580 --> 02:04:57.020
we'll travel to the unknown west.

1265
02:04:57.740 --> 02:04:58.700
and we will honor him.

1266
02:04:59.260 --> 02:05:00.780
These men were sent by the suitors.

1267
02:05:01.420 --> 02:05:02.380
There will be more of them

1268
02:05:02.380 --> 02:05:03.740
we need to get on the road.

1269
02:05:03.740 --> 02:05:04.700
By night?

1270
02:05:04.700 --> 02:05:05.740
Safer than staying here.

1271
02:05:06.300 --> 02:05:08.460
Mentor's at the heart of Athena's oldest temple.

1272
02:05:09.260 --> 02:05:11.660
For now. we'll honor him in flame.

1273
02:05:11.660 --> 02:05:52.300
How does a beggar come to know Mentor?

1274
02:05:52.300 --> 02:05:53.420
I told you.

1275
02:05:53.420 --> 02:05:54.620
I wasn't always a beggar.

1276
02:05:55.260 --> 02:05:56.380
You have wise eyes.

1277
02:05:57.340 --> 02:05:58.060
Athena's eyes-

1278
02:05:58.060 --> 02:05:59.900
Don't look for gods in men.

1279
02:05:59.900 --> 02:06:01.180
You'll just be disappointed.

1280
02:06:01.660 --> 02:06:03.020
Well. then tell me who you are.

1281
02:06:05.100 --> 02:06:06.140
My name is Sinon.

1282
02:06:07.100 --> 02:06:09.820
I'm an old soldier who fought under your father at Troy.

1283
02:06:11.260 --> 02:06:12.140
You knew my father?

1284
02:06:12.780 --> 02:06:14.220
I've been taking orders from him

1285
02:06:14.220 --> 02:06:15.740
for longer than I care to remember.

1286
02:06:16.940 --> 02:06:17.820
Wait. he's alive?

1287
02:06:19.260 --> 02:06:19.820
He's alive.

1288
02:06:21.820 --> 02:06:22.780
Well. where is he?

1289
02:06:23.500 --> 02:06:24.380
He's close by.

1290
02:06:25.100 --> 02:06:27.420
He sent me ahead to assess the situation.

1291
02:06:29.180 --> 02:06:31.020
Well. Mother's not gonna believe.

1292
02:06:31.020 --> 02:06:32.060
No. you can't tell her.

1293
02:06:33.420 --> 02:06:34.140
What. nothing?

1294
02:06:35.740 --> 02:06:36.240
Tell her.

1295
02:06:37.980 --> 02:06:40.060
Tell her he decided it's time she remarried.

1296
02:06:42.180 --> 02:06:43.540
He doesn't need to test my mother.

1297
02:06:43.540 --> 02:06:44.740
How can he doubt her?

1298
02:06:44.740 --> 02:06:45.380
He doesn't.

1299
02:06:46.260 --> 02:06:48.420
But she has every reason to have forgotten him.

1300
02:06:50.450 --> 02:06:51.890
Are there any loyal servants?

1301
02:06:53.250 --> 02:06:53.750
Some.

1302
02:06:55.010 --> 02:06:56.770
Well. at least he shows himself off.

1303
02:06:58.530 --> 02:07:00.450
We need to gather the suitors at the palace.

1304
02:07:01.490 --> 02:07:02.610
That's never a problem.

1305
02:07:03.490 --> 02:07:04.770
You have gifts for Menelaus?

1306
02:07:05.570 --> 02:07:06.290
Yeah. on my ship.

1307
02:07:07.170 --> 02:07:07.670
Good.

1308
02:07:08.610 --> 02:07:09.570
Come back as a prince.

1309
02:07:10.210 --> 02:07:11.090
Call a banquet.

1310
02:07:11.970 --> 02:07:12.610
I'll be there.

1311
02:07:13.970 --> 02:07:15.250
What will you do at the banquet?

1312
02:07:16.210 --> 02:07:16.710
Beg.

1313
02:07:17.650 --> 02:07:19.570
The clearest view of a man is from below.

1314
02:07:21.760 --> 02:07:22.960
How many suitors are there?

1315
02:07:24.000 --> 02:07:24.500
Dozens.

1316
02:07:25.520 --> 02:07:27.200
If all of his men are like you. then...

1317
02:07:27.200 --> 02:07:28.320
He doesn't have any men.

1318
02:07:29.520 --> 02:07:30.240
But it's just you?

1319
02:07:32.000 --> 02:07:32.500
And you.

1320
02:07:40.220 --> 02:07:40.720
What?

1321
02:07:40.720 --> 02:07:41.580
Let me kiss his back.

1322
02:07:42.460 --> 02:07:43.740
Who's seeing this?

1323
02:07:43.740 --> 02:07:45.500
Everyone's seeing it.

1324
02:07:45.500 --> 02:07:46.380
Where's Telemachus?

1325
02:07:47.180 --> 02:07:48.220
My queen!

1326
02:07:48.220 --> 02:07:49.660
Surely your place is up there.

1327
02:08:02.160 --> 02:08:02.660
You're home.

1328
02:08:04.160 --> 02:08:04.660
I'm back.

1329
02:08:05.600 --> 02:08:07.040
I thought tonight we could celebrate.

1330
02:08:08.240 --> 02:08:10.880
If we have anything left to celebrate with.

1331
02:08:12.720 --> 02:08:13.280
Don't worry.

1332
02:08:14.160 --> 02:08:17.360
My brave suitors will shower us with gifts to honor your return.

1333
02:08:17.360 --> 02:08:18.880
So glad to see your safe return.

1334
02:08:29.980 --> 02:08:31.100
Glad to see you on your feet.

1335
02:08:31.820 --> 02:08:33.740
It's about time we took you back to the palace.

1336
02:08:34.140 --> 02:08:41.150
Did you hear about your father?

1337
02:08:41.150 --> 02:08:41.650
Nothing.

1338
02:08:42.990 --> 02:08:46.270
Stories from the war. but no one knew what happened to him after he left Troy.

1339
02:08:46.990 --> 02:08:47.790
I'm sorry. Mom.

1340
02:08:49.230 --> 02:08:52.590
Everyone in Sparta is talking about a coming catastrophe.

1341
02:08:53.630 --> 02:08:57.630
An age of darkness brought on by people from the sea. violating Zeus's law.

1342
02:08:57.630 --> 02:08:58.990
Some stories are true.

1343
02:08:58.990 --> 02:09:00.750
Our civilization is collapsing.

1344
02:09:01.310 --> 02:09:04.110
Menelaus is concerned that we can't raise an army with an empty throne.

1345
02:09:04.750 --> 02:09:05.790
So we came up with a plan.

1346
02:09:07.710 --> 02:09:08.270
What plan?

1347
02:09:10.880 --> 02:09:12.480
The one way to get the suitors to leave.

1348
02:09:15.200 --> 02:09:16.320
It's time for you to choose.

1349
02:09:20.560 --> 02:09:21.680
You want me to remarry?

1350
02:09:22.320 --> 02:09:25.200
If you remarry. the other suitors will leave.

1351
02:09:25.200 --> 02:09:27.200
And then I'm only dealing with one of them.

1352
02:09:27.200 --> 02:09:28.240
I'm almost of age.

1353
02:09:28.240 --> 02:09:31.200
I'll tell the elders that I learned of Odysseus's death in Sparta.

1354
02:09:32.160 --> 02:09:35.280
Menelaus will back me up and they'll let me assume the throne.

1355
02:09:35.280 --> 02:09:38.800
So you deny your father and I leave my home and go live with one of these snakes.

1356
02:09:38.800 --> 02:09:39.920
That's your plan.

1357
02:09:39.920 --> 02:09:40.960
Would you have a better one?

1358
02:09:44.190 --> 02:09:47.230
Lock the door. set a fire. and burn the bastards.

1359
02:09:48.590 --> 02:09:51.950
Do you think if I remarry. life goes back to the way it was?

1360
02:09:53.310 --> 02:09:55.230
That world is gone.

1361
02:09:55.230 --> 02:09:59.790
And in this one. they're not giving up power to you or Odysseus.

1362
02:09:59.790 --> 02:10:01.550
If he returned. he'd have to kill him.

1363
02:10:02.270 --> 02:10:02.990
Like you will.

1364
02:10:04.190 --> 02:10:07.150
If I kill them. their families will demand revenge.

1365
02:10:07.150 --> 02:10:08.190
I'd be exiled.

1366
02:10:09.390 --> 02:10:11.230
And without me. the throne will be lost.

1367
02:10:11.230 --> 02:10:12.750
Because only a man can sit on it.

1368
02:10:15.010 --> 02:10:16.210
Empty throne?

1369
02:10:16.210 --> 02:10:20.300
I've been sitting on that empty throne for 20 years.

1370
02:10:21.340 --> 02:10:24.140
My knowledge. my years of experience.

1371
02:10:24.140 --> 02:10:26.300
that's all nothing compared with the bristles on your chin.

1372
02:10:29.490 --> 02:10:30.370
Do you want peace?

1373
02:10:31.890 --> 02:10:32.770
Or do you want revenge?

1374
02:10:32.770 --> 02:10:34.290
I want Odysseus!

1375
02:10:34.290 --> 02:10:37.860
Sing!

1376
02:10:38.880 --> 02:10:39.920
Muse of a face!

1377
02:10:40.640 --> 02:10:44.370
A face!

1378
02:10:54.850 --> 02:10:55.410
A war!

1379
02:11:00.140 --> 02:11:00.860
At Troy!

1380
02:11:05.520 --> 02:11:06.880
Sing Muse of a man!

1381
02:11:07.520 --> 02:11:27.840
Man. go ahead.

1382
02:11:28.400 --> 02:12:00.510
You should bury him up on the cliff.

1383
02:12:03.010 --> 02:12:13.870
Where you met your Odysseus.

1384
02:12:17.390 --> 02:12:18.350
You're my father.

1385
02:12:18.910 --> 02:12:22.210
I'm a beggar.

1386
02:12:22.690 --> 02:12:34.910
And you are the worthy of Odysseus.

1387
02:12:34.910 --> 02:12:42.990
Of whom. when you do finally meet. he will be so proud.

1388
02:12:47.380 --> 02:12:48.100
Well. come on.

1389
02:12:49.140 --> 02:12:49.780
Stranger.

1390
02:12:53.410 --> 02:12:55.010
Because the darkness of victory

1391
02:12:56.370 --> 02:12:57.970
shrouds the rightist allegiance

1392
02:13:00.430 --> 02:13:02.750
and make bars to blind beggars of us all.

1393
02:13:03.310 --> 02:13:12.400
Accept the hospitality of Zeus.

1394
02:13:13.120 --> 02:13:14.400
Get the stranger some food.

1395
02:13:15.040 --> 02:13:15.540
No. sir.

1396
02:13:16.800 --> 02:13:18.400
I'll just make the rounds with a bowl.

1397
02:13:24.220 --> 02:13:26.940
Had to invite another one of these filthy plate lickers today.

1398
02:13:26.940 --> 02:13:28.620
What's one more beggar at this feast?

1399
02:13:46.990 --> 02:13:49.790
We need to kill him before he regains his position with the elders.

1400
02:13:49.790 --> 02:13:50.290
How?

1401
02:14:07.660 --> 02:14:08.300
Provoke him.

1402
02:14:09.260 --> 02:14:10.620
Get him to grab one of the weapons.

1403
02:14:15.340 --> 02:14:17.740
He's not the same young pup he used to be.

1404
02:14:18.300 --> 02:14:19.660
He may come back tonight.

1405
02:14:19.660 --> 02:14:20.620
Let me cut his tongue.

1406
02:14:22.060 --> 02:14:23.020
Fuck off. old man.

1407
02:14:23.580 --> 02:14:24.080
Please.

1408
02:14:24.620 --> 02:14:25.900
It's been days since I've eaten.

1409
02:14:25.900 --> 02:14:27.100
I told you to fuck off.

1410
02:14:27.100 --> 02:14:30.060
You can't be generous with food that's not even yours.

1411
02:14:32.300 --> 02:14:33.900
There's no room in your bowl.

1412
02:14:33.900 --> 02:14:34.540
Yes. there is.

1413
02:14:40.820 --> 02:14:41.540
So there is.

1414
02:14:43.630 --> 02:14:44.110
There you go.

1415
02:14:46.990 --> 02:14:50.700
I suppose you want something from me now.

1416
02:14:50.700 --> 02:14:51.200
No.

1417
02:14:52.300 --> 02:14:53.820
I have something for you.

1418
02:14:54.940 --> 02:14:55.980
You have something for me?

1419
02:14:57.420 --> 02:14:58.540
You have something for me!

1420
02:15:00.780 --> 02:15:02.220
What could a filthy old

1421
02:15:02.860 --> 02:15:06.620
plate licker like you possibly have that I could want?

1422
02:15:07.500 --> 02:15:09.500
Actually. it's yours.

1423
02:15:15.390 --> 02:15:19.310
I was asked to return it.

1424
02:15:19.310 --> 02:15:20.030
It's not mine.

1425
02:15:22.300 --> 02:15:22.800
No?

1426
02:15:26.740 --> 02:15:27.460
Where did you get this?

1427
02:15:30.700 --> 02:15:31.900
Turn your back on me!

1428
02:15:37.380 --> 02:15:38.020
Continuous.

1429
02:15:38.980 --> 02:15:40.500
You can't treat a beggar like that.

1430
02:15:40.500 --> 02:15:41.380
It's Zeus's law.

1431
02:15:42.340 --> 02:15:43.060
Zeus's law.

1432
02:15:47.950 --> 02:15:48.990
You think this is a god?

1433
02:15:52.900 --> 02:15:54.340
You think this is a god in disguise?

1434
02:15:55.700 --> 02:15:56.200
Are you?

1435
02:15:58.400 --> 02:15:59.200
Are you a god?

1436
02:16:00.880 --> 02:16:01.520
Plate licker?

1437
02:16:04.750 --> 02:16:06.510
I'm a veteran of the Trojan War.

1438
02:16:07.390 --> 02:16:09.150
A veteran of the Trojan War.

1439
02:16:12.110 --> 02:16:13.390
And what's your name. veteran?

1440
02:16:18.180 --> 02:16:18.680
Synod.

1441
02:16:22.510 --> 02:16:23.950
Does that name mean something to you?

1442
02:16:25.470 --> 02:16:25.970
No.

1443
02:16:27.710 --> 02:16:31.310
I use it to honor the bravest young man I ever met.

1444
02:16:32.190 --> 02:16:32.690
Synod?

1445
02:16:34.690 --> 02:16:36.450
I'm not just Shepard's boy.

1446
02:16:36.450 --> 02:16:40.100
I don't remember that.

1447
02:16:40.100 --> 02:16:42.420
Yeah. yeah. he wanted you to take his place.

1448
02:16:43.220 --> 02:16:44.180
Where does he find us?

1449
02:16:45.060 --> 02:16:46.100
Where he'll soon be.

1450
02:16:47.060 --> 02:16:48.180
And where is that?

1451
02:16:49.620 --> 02:16:50.120
Hades.

1452
02:17:05.040 --> 02:17:06.320
It's not mine!

1453
02:17:06.320 --> 02:17:17.100
A queen would like to speak with you.

1454
02:17:17.100 --> 02:17:19.340
Stay till everyone's left and the fire's on them.

1455
02:17:53.260 --> 02:18:14.340
Hello. traveler.

1456
02:18:14.340 --> 02:18:17.620
Melanthal. have your clay wash the stranger's feet.

1457
02:18:18.180 --> 02:18:19.540
That's not necessary.

1458
02:18:19.540 --> 02:18:22.980
My husband insisted we honor guests as family.

1459
02:18:23.860 --> 02:18:25.620
You know of a disease. my husband?

1460
02:18:26.420 --> 02:18:26.920
Of course.

1461
02:18:27.860 --> 02:18:28.580
Of the souls.

1462
02:18:29.540 --> 02:18:30.900
I was at Troy. ma'am.

1463
02:18:31.780 --> 02:18:33.700
And the songs probably seem selling.

1464
02:18:35.380 --> 02:18:36.340
They make me cry.

1465
02:18:37.940 --> 02:18:38.440
Why?

1466
02:18:39.540 --> 02:18:40.580
For what was lost.

1467
02:18:41.860 --> 02:18:42.420
The lives?

1468
02:18:43.700 --> 02:18:44.420
And the years.

1469
02:18:46.340 --> 02:18:54.930
Everything.

1470
02:18:54.930 --> 02:18:56.370
Did you know it was just at Troy?

1471
02:18:57.410 --> 02:18:57.910
I did.

1472
02:18:59.330 --> 02:19:02.370
Was there some detail of how he dressed?

1473
02:19:02.370 --> 02:19:03.170
Something he wore?

1474
02:19:04.130 --> 02:19:05.490
He wore a strange pin.

1475
02:19:08.450 --> 02:19:08.930
Beautiful.

1476
02:19:10.050 --> 02:19:10.930
But out of place.

1477
02:19:11.970 --> 02:19:13.010
Describe it.

1478
02:19:13.010 --> 02:19:28.850
I'll get more water.

1479
02:19:28.850 --> 02:19:48.700
It was a beautiful figure of Athena encircled by vines.

1480
02:19:48.700 --> 02:19:49.900
Did he wear it often?

1481
02:19:51.660 --> 02:19:52.780
He never took it off.

1482
02:20:13.230 --> 02:20:15.710
How is it that you made it home when Odysseus could not?

1483
02:20:17.230 --> 02:20:18.030
I'm not home.

1484
02:20:18.990 --> 02:20:19.490
Not yet.

1485
02:20:20.830 --> 02:20:21.330
Why not?

1486
02:20:22.190 --> 02:20:24.590
It's not always an easy thing. a homecoming.

1487
02:20:25.550 --> 02:20:28.110
That's as true for Odysseus as for me.

1488
02:20:28.110 --> 02:20:36.640
The Odysseus I knew would have found or fought his way back to me no matter what.

1489
02:20:37.760 --> 02:20:40.880
What if the Odysseus you knew lost his way?

1490
02:20:45.820 --> 02:20:57.820
What if one night in a strange city he saw things that made him think the home

1491
02:20:57.820 --> 02:21:06.110
he knew couldn't possibly be there anymore?

1492
02:21:06.190 --> 02:21:15.760
What if when he left the belly of the horse and opened the gates of Troy

1493
02:21:15.760 --> 02:21:25.950
he saw ten years of rage pour into that city

1494
02:21:25.950 --> 02:26:58.030
and one night if he knew exactly what he'd done the sea spreading like

1495
02:26:58.030 --> 02:27:01.310
and maybe he couldn't bear to see the ruins of what he'd done.

1496
02:27:01.310 --> 02:27:21.070
But Odysseus is alive and he'll be here when you need him most.

1497
02:27:21.070 --> 02:27:27.970
I've been living in his ruins long enough.

1498
02:27:33.980 --> 02:27:35.500
Bring the shooters at dawn.

1499
02:27:35.500 --> 02:27:36.780
Remove the arms from the hall.

1500
02:27:37.340 --> 02:27:41.980
In a few hours one suitor will be happy and the rest angry vengeful men.

1501
02:27:41.980 --> 02:27:46.060
Ron's draws a man on so we'll have no weapons at the trial and the doors will be locked.

1502
02:27:46.860 --> 02:27:49.340
I'll marry whoever completes my challenge.

1503
02:27:49.340 --> 02:27:57.280
How will we take on the suitors?

1504
02:27:58.480 --> 02:27:58.980
Not we.

1505
02:28:00.160 --> 02:28:04.000
Their blood belongs on my hands or you'd face exile.

1506
02:28:05.280 --> 02:28:05.780
Won't you?

1507
02:28:07.280 --> 02:28:09.680
Just make sure they can't get those weapons.

1508
02:28:09.680 --> 02:28:10.480
I'll see to it.

1509
02:28:11.440 --> 02:28:16.080
Even an arm there are so many of them and you won't have a weapon.

1510
02:28:16.080 --> 02:28:18.700
Well I'll have a weapon.

1511
02:28:18.700 --> 02:28:21.940
Your mother's Saint Devat.

1512
02:28:21.940 --> 02:28:26.480
Gather the servants you trust.

1513
02:28:26.480 --> 02:28:28.480
Lock the arms in the upper storage room.

1514
02:28:30.000 --> 02:29:23.950
She seems interested in the outcome.

1515
02:29:23.950 --> 02:29:25.070
What was the trial?

1516
02:29:52.220 --> 02:29:57.260
While my husband's been gone you've commandeered this palace for your feasting.

1517
02:29:58.140 --> 02:30:01.100
Your excuse was you wanted to win my hand in marriage.

1518
02:30:04.980 --> 02:30:09.520
As I listened to you party and fight.

1519
02:30:09.520 --> 02:30:15.070
corrupt my servants and disrespect my home.

1520
02:30:15.710 --> 02:30:19.630
I wanted nothing more than for Odysseus to return and see all this for himself.

1521
02:30:20.270 --> 02:30:31.310
But last night I realized he's not here now.

1522
02:30:34.880 --> 02:30:35.840
He never will be.

1523
02:30:39.950 --> 02:30:45.790
I stand ready to marry anyone here who can string this hunting bow

1524
02:30:45.790 --> 02:30:48.270
and shoot an arrow through those 12 axes.

1525
02:30:53.650 --> 02:30:54.690
And show me who you are.

1526
02:30:56.530 --> 02:30:57.090
Eumaeus.

1527
02:31:09.310 --> 02:31:10.190
Who's first?

1528
02:31:10.910 --> 02:31:12.270
Give me the bow.

1529
02:31:12.270 --> 02:31:33.920
It can't be done.

1530
02:31:33.920 --> 02:31:34.420
By you.

1531
02:31:35.200 --> 02:31:37.120
As a boy I saw Odysseus do it with ease.

1532
02:31:37.840 --> 02:31:38.560
Then you do it.

1533
02:31:39.600 --> 02:31:40.480
I'll wait my turn.

1534
02:31:41.760 --> 02:31:43.760
Why don't we warm the bow and oil it?

1535
02:31:45.360 --> 02:31:47.200
This bow hasn't been strung in years.

1536
02:32:20.900 --> 02:32:21.400
Your turn.

1537
02:32:22.340 --> 02:32:23.460
There's no need.

1538
02:32:23.460 --> 02:32:27.760
The Queen has made her point.

1539
02:32:28.640 --> 02:32:29.920
We can't replace Odysseus.

1540
02:32:30.560 --> 02:32:32.080
Some of us have always known that.

1541
02:32:32.080 --> 02:32:35.680
Some of us would have followed that great man. Detroit. if he'd let us.

1542
02:32:38.860 --> 02:32:40.060
But he never came back.

1543
02:32:42.370 --> 02:32:43.650
So choose the best of us here.

1544
02:32:45.820 --> 02:32:47.660
All of us who failed your test.

1545
02:32:48.460 --> 02:32:49.020
I haven't.

1546
02:32:54.620 --> 02:32:56.220
Somebody get these beggars out of here!

1547
02:32:57.340 --> 02:33:00.140
The doors are locked until the trial's over.

1548
02:33:00.140 --> 02:33:01.660
The trial is over.

1549
02:33:02.940 --> 02:33:03.740
I want to try.

1550
02:33:04.380 --> 02:33:05.180
Give it a rest.

1551
02:33:07.660 --> 02:33:09.020
He has a right to try.

1552
02:33:10.460 --> 02:33:12.460
If he won't. he may as well.

1553
02:33:13.020 --> 02:33:15.100
You're making a mockery of your mother's wishes.

1554
02:33:15.100 --> 02:33:17.500
My mother's wishes are for you all to burn.

1555
02:33:36.430 --> 02:33:38.110
Look at the expert at work.

1556
02:33:43.020 --> 02:33:44.060
Old man. forget it!

1557
02:33:45.420 --> 02:33:58.220
That's enough.

1558
02:34:52.860 --> 02:42:07.900
Centuries of darkness to follow.

1559
02:42:08.780 --> 02:42:12.620
The stories of Troy will only be sung.

1560
02:42:12.620 --> 02:43:58.100
You can't. Not now. Not now. please.

1561
02:43:58.100 --> 02:44:43.920
Why will the stories only be sung?

1562
02:44:47.150 --> 02:44:50.510
Because songs will be all they have to remember those of us who could write.

1563
02:44:55.740 --> 02:44:57.820
Civilization will rise again.

1564
02:44:57.820 --> 02:45:06.750
A new dawn will break over the darkened world.

1565
02:45:06.750 --> 02:45:12.960
And our mistakes will once again be forgotten.

1566
02:45:13.305 --> 02:46:13.760
